          {
            "heading": "Part D: Teesra niyam: T² ∝ r³",
            "headingHinglish": "Part D: Teesra niyam: T² ∝ r³",
            "body": "> **Kepler ka teesra niyam:** Planet ke ek chakkar ke time T ka **square**, Sooraj se uski average doori r (yani a) ke **cube** ke anupaat mein hota hai: **T² ∝ r³**.\n\nDo planets ke liye: **T₁² ÷ T₂² = r₁³ ÷ r₂³**.\n\nAb Sooraj ke planets ke liye ek **seedhi list** dekhte hain. Agar T saal mein aur r AU mein naapein, to Earth ke liye T = 1 saal aur r = 1 AU, to T² ÷ r³ = 1 ÷ 1 = 1. Baaki planets bhi lagbhag yahi number dete hain:\n\n| Planet | r (AU) | T (saal) | T² | r³ | T² ÷ r³ |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Mercury | 0.39 | 0.24 | 0.058 | 0.059 | 0.97 |\n| Venus | 0.72 | 0.62 | 0.38 | 0.37 | 1.03 |\n| Earth | 1.00 | 1.00 | 1.00 | 1.00 | 1.00 |\n| Mars | 1.52 | 1.88 | 3.53 | 3.51 | 1.01 |\n| Jupiter | 5.20 | 11.9 | 141.6 | 140.6 | 1.01 |\n| Saturn | 9.58 | 29.5 | 870 | 879 | 0.99 |\n| Uranus | 19.2 | 84 | 7056 | 7078 | 1.00 |\n| Neptune | 30.1 | 165 | 27225 | 27271 | 1.00 |\n\nAathon planets ke liye T² ÷ r³ lagbhag 1 aata hai (thoda sa farak sirf diye gaye numbers ke rounding ka hai). **Ye wahi niyam hai jo Kepler ne Tycho ke numbers se pakda tha.**\n\nIs niyam se do baatein nikalti hain:\n- Jo planet Sooraj se door hai, uska chakkar **zyada lamba** hota hai: sirf isliye nahi ki raasta bada hai, balki isliye bhi ki wo **dheere** chalta hai (Part E mein ye dekhenge).\n- Agar ek planet ki doori aur time pata ho, aur doosre ki sirf ek cheez, to doosri cheez nikal sakti hai.\n\n**Example 6 (doori se time: Mercury):** Mercury ki Sooraj se average doori 0.39 AU hai. Uska time period T nikalo (saal aur din mein).\n\n- T² = r³ = 0.39³ = 0.0593 (T saal mein, r AU mein)\n- T = √0.0593 = **0.24 saal** (lagbhag; 0.2436)\n- Dinon mein: 0.2436 × 365 = **lagbhag 89 din**\n\n**Jaanch:** 0.2436² = 0.0593 ✅. Mercury ka saal Earth ke lagbhag teen mahine se bhi kam hota hai.\n\n**Example 7 (kalpit planet, doori 4 AU):** Ek kalpit planet X Sooraj se 4 AU door hai. Uska T nikalo.\n\n- T² = r³ = 4³ = 64, to T = √64 = **8 saal**\n\n**Jaanch:** Earth se compare: T₁² ÷ T₂² = r₁³ ÷ r₂³ = 64 ÷ 1 = 64, to T₁ ÷ T₂ = 8 ✅. Doori Earth ki 4 guna hai, par chakkar 4 guna nahi, **8 guna** lamba hai.\n\n**Example 8 (time se doori):** Ek kalpit planet Y ka time period 27 saal hai. Sooraj se uski doori AU mein nikalo.\n\n- r³ = T² = 27² = 729, to r = ∛729 = **9 AU**\n\n**Jaanch:** 9³ = 729 = 27² ✅. Ye planet Earth se 9 guna door hai (Saturn ke aas-paas).\n\nChhoti trick: jab r = 4, 9 jaise \"achhe\" numbers hon to T = r × √r hota hai: r = 4 → 4 × 2 = 8; r = 9 → 9 × 3 = 27. Aur r = 1 → T = 1.",
            "bodyHinglish": "> **Kepler ka teesra niyam:** Planet ke ek chakkar ke time T ka **square**, Sooraj se uski average doori r (yani a) ke **cube** ke anupaat mein hota hai: **T² ∝ r³**.\n\nDo planets ke liye: **T₁² ÷ T₂² = r₁³ ÷ r₂³**.\n\nAb Sooraj ke planets ke liye ek **seedhi list** dekhte hain. Agar T saal mein aur r AU mein naapein, to Earth ke liye T = 1 saal aur r = 1 AU, to T² ÷ r³ = 1 ÷ 1 = 1. Baaki planets bhi lagbhag yahi number dete hain:\n\n| Planet | r (AU) | T (saal) | T² | r³ | T² ÷ r³ |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Mercury | 0.39 | 0.24 | 0.058 | 0.059 | 0.97 |\n| Venus | 0.72 | 0.62 | 0.38 | 0.37 | 1.03 |\n| Earth | 1.00 | 1.00 | 1.00 | 1.00 | 1.00 |\n| Mars | 1.52 | 1.88 | 3.53 | 3.51 | 1.01 |\n| Jupiter | 5.20 | 11.9 | 141.6 | 140.6 | 1.01 |\n| Saturn | 9.58 | 29.5 | 870 | 879 | 0.99 |\n| Uranus | 19.2 | 84 | 7056 | 7078 | 1.00 |\n| Neptune | 30.1 | 165 | 27225 | 27271 | 1.00 |\n\nAathon planets ke liye T² ÷ r³ lagbhag 1 aata hai (thoda sa farak sirf diye gaye numbers ke rounding ka hai). **Ye wahi niyam hai jo Kepler ne Tycho ke numbers se pakda tha.**\n\nIs niyam se do baatein nikalti hain:\n- Jo planet Sooraj se door hai, uska chakkar **zyada lamba** hota hai: sirf isliye nahi ki raasta bada hai, balki isliye bhi ki wo **dheere** chalta hai (Part E mein ye dekhenge).\n- Agar ek planet ki doori aur time pata ho, aur doosre ki sirf ek cheez, to doosri cheez nikal sakti hai.\n\n**Example 6 (doori se time: Mercury):** Mercury ki Sooraj se average doori 0.39 AU hai. Uska time period T nikalo (saal aur din mein).\n\n- T² = r³ = 0.39³ = 0.0593 (T saal mein, r AU mein)\n- T = √0.0593 = **0.24 saal** (lagbhag; 0.2436)\n- Dinon mein: 0.2436 × 365 = **lagbhag 89 din**\n\n**Jaanch:** 0.2436² = 0.0593 ✅. Mercury ka saal Earth ke lagbhag teen mahine se bhi kam hota hai.\n\n**Example 7 (kalpit planet, doori 4 AU):** Ek kalpit planet X Sooraj se 4 AU door hai. Uska T nikalo.\n\n- T² = r³ = 4³ = 64, to T = √64 = **8 saal**\n\n**Jaanch:** Earth se compare: T₁² ÷ T₂² = r₁³ ÷ r₂³ = 64 ÷ 1 = 64, to T₁ ÷ T₂ = 8 ✅. Doori Earth ki 4 guna hai, par chakkar 4 guna nahi, **8 guna** lamba hai.\n\n**Example 8 (time se doori):** Ek kalpit planet Y ka time period 27 saal hai. Sooraj se uski doori AU mein nikalo.\n\n- r³ = T² = 27² = 729, to r = ∛729 = **9 AU**\n\n**Jaanch:** 9³ = 729 = 27² ✅. Ye planet Earth se 9 guna door hai (Saturn ke aas-paas).\n\nChhoti trick: jab r = 4, 9 jaise \"achhe\" numbers hon to T = r × √r hota hai: r = 4 → 4 × 2 = 8; r = 9 → 9 × 3 = 27. Aur r = 1 → T = 1."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part E: Kepler ke niyam satellite par bhi chalte hain",
            "headingHinglish": "Part E: Kepler ke niyam satellite par bhi chalte hain",
            "body": "Kepler ne niyam planets ke liye nikaale the, par ye **kisi bhi cheez ke liye** sach hain jo gravity ke asar mein kisi bade pind ke chaaro taraf ghoomti hai: Sooraj ke planets, Earth ka Chaand, Earth ke artificial satellite, Jupiter ke chaand (sirf naam). Class 9 ke Newton ke cannon wale satellite bhi ellipse (ya lagbhag circle) mein chalte hain, **Earth ek focus par**. Ek farak zaroor hai: Sooraj ke planets ke liye T² ÷ r³ ek hi number hai, aur **Earth ke satellites ke liye ek doosra number**. Kyun, ye Topic 4 mein orbit ke formula se saaf ho jaayega.\n\n**Example 9 (Chaand ke saath satellite):** Chaand ki orbit ka radius lagbhag 3.84 × 10⁸ m aur time period 27.3 din hai. Ek satellite Earth ke chaaro taraf 9.6 × 10⁷ m radius ke circle mein chalta hai (Chaand ke radius ka chauthai hissa). Uska time period nikalo.\n\n- Radius ka anupaat: 9.6 × 10⁷ ÷ 3.84 × 10⁸ = 1/4\n- (T_sat ÷ 27.3 din)² = (1/4)³ = 1/64, to T_sat ÷ 27.3 din = 1/8\n- T_sat = 27.3 din ÷ 8 = **3.41 din** (lagbhag; yani 81.9 ghante)\n\n**Jaanch:** (3.41 ÷ 27.3)² = (0.125)² = 0.0156 aur (1/4)³ = 0.0156 ✅. Chaand ki orbit ka chauthai radius, par time 8 guna kam: Class 9 ka pattern \"paas wala kam time lega\" sach nikla.\n\n**Example 10 (Earth ke satellites ka constant):** (a) Chaand ke data (T = 2.36 × 10⁶ s, r = 3.84 × 10⁸ m) se T² ÷ r³ nikalo. (b) Ek geostationary satellite ka radius 4.2 × 10⁷ m hai (Earth ke centre se); uska T lagbhag 24 ghante hona chahiye. Kepler ke niyam se jaanch karo.\n\n(a) T² = (2.36 × 10⁶ s)² = 5.57 × 10¹² s²; r³ = (3.84 × 10⁸ m)³ = 5.66 × 10²⁵ m³. T² ÷ r³ = 5.57 × 10¹² ÷ 5.66 × 10²⁵ = **9.8 × 10⁻¹⁴ s²/m³** (Earth ke satellites ke liye).\n\n(b) Ratio se: T ÷ 27.3 din = (4.2 × 10⁷ ÷ 3.84 × 10⁸)^(3/2) = (0.109)^1.5 = 0.0362, to T = 27.3 × 0.0362 = 0.988 din = **23.7 ghante** (lagbhag).\n\n**Jaanch:** 23.7 ghante lagbhag 24 ghante ke bahut paas hai (4.2 × 10⁷ m ek rounded value hai) ✅. Seedha constant se bhi: T = 86,160 s (23 h 56 min) aur r = 4.2 × 10⁷ m ke liye T² ÷ r³ = 7.42 × 10⁹ ÷ 7.41 × 10²² = 1.0 × 10⁻¹³ s²/m³, jo (a) ke 9.8 × 10⁻¹⁴ ke bahut paas hai ✅. Dekho, ek Chaand aur ek kritrim satellite, dono ek hi niyam par chalte hain.\n\nSooraj ke planets ke liye isi constant ka maan SI units mein lagbhag 2.9 × 10⁻¹⁹ s²/m³ hai (Earth: T = 3.15 × 10⁷ s, r = 1.5 × 10¹¹ m). Alag number, kyunki ghoomne wale ke beech ka \"kendra\" alag hai (Sooraj ya Earth).",
            "bodyHinglish": "Kepler ne niyam planets ke liye nikaale the, par ye **kisi bhi cheez ke liye** sach hain jo gravity ke asar mein kisi bade pind ke chaaro taraf ghoomti hai: Sooraj ke planets, Earth ka Chaand, Earth ke artificial satellite, Jupiter ke chaand (sirf naam). Class 9 ke Newton ke cannon wale satellite bhi ellipse (ya lagbhag circle) mein chalte hain, **Earth ek focus par**. Ek farak zaroor hai: Sooraj ke planets ke liye T² ÷ r³ ek hi number hai, aur **Earth ke satellites ke liye ek doosra number**. Kyun, ye Topic 4 mein orbit ke formula se saaf ho jaayega.\n\n**Example 9 (Chaand ke saath satellite):** Chaand ki orbit ka radius lagbhag 3.84 × 10⁸ m aur time period 27.3 din hai. Ek satellite Earth ke chaaro taraf 9.6 × 10⁷ m radius ke circle mein chalta hai (Chaand ke radius ka chauthai hissa). Uska time period nikalo.\n\n- Radius ka anupaat: 9.6 × 10⁷ ÷ 3.84 × 10⁸ = 1/4\n- (T_sat ÷ 27.3 din)² = (1/4)³ = 1/64, to T_sat ÷ 27.3 din = 1/8\n- T_sat = 27.3 din ÷ 8 = **3.41 din** (lagbhag; yani 81.9 ghante)\n\n**Jaanch:** (3.41 ÷ 27.3)² = (0.125)² = 0.0156 aur (1/4)³ = 0.0156 ✅. Chaand ki orbit ka chauthai radius, par time 8 guna kam: Class 9 ka pattern \"paas wala kam time lega\" sach nikla.\n\n**Example 10 (Earth ke satellites ka constant):** (a) Chaand ke data (T = 2.36 × 10⁶ s, r = 3.84 × 10⁸ m) se T² ÷ r³ nikalo. (b) Ek geostationary satellite ka radius 4.2 × 10⁷ m hai (Earth ke centre se); uska T lagbhag 24 ghante hona chahiye. Kepler ke niyam se jaanch karo.\n\n(a) T² = (2.36 × 10⁶ s)² = 5.57 × 10¹² s²; r³ = (3.84 × 10⁸ m)³ = 5.66 × 10²⁵ m³. T² ÷ r³ = 5.57 × 10¹² ÷ 5.66 × 10²⁵ = **9.8 × 10⁻¹⁴ s²/m³** (Earth ke satellites ke liye).\n\n(b) Ratio se: T ÷ 27.3 din = (4.2 × 10⁷ ÷ 3.84 × 10⁸)^(3/2) = (0.109)^1.5 = 0.0362, to T = 27.3 × 0.0362 = 0.988 din = **23.7 ghante** (lagbhag).\n\n**Jaanch:** 23.7 ghante lagbhag 24 ghante ke bahut paas hai (4.2 × 10⁷ m ek rounded value hai) ✅. Seedha constant se bhi: T = 86,160 s (23 h 56 min) aur r = 4.2 × 10⁷ m ke liye T² ÷ r³ = 7.42 × 10⁹ ÷ 7.41 × 10²² = 1.0 × 10⁻¹³ s²/m³, jo (a) ke 9.8 × 10⁻¹⁴ ke bahut paas hai ✅. Dekho, ek Chaand aur ek kritrim satellite, dono ek hi niyam par chalte hain.\n\nSooraj ke planets ke liye isi constant ka maan SI units mein lagbhag 2.9 × 10⁻¹⁹ s²/m³ hai (Earth: T = 3.15 × 10⁷ s, r = 1.5 × 10¹¹ m). Alag number, kyunki ghoomne wale ke beech ka \"kendra\" alag hai (Sooraj ya Earth)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part F: Teeno niyam ek saath",
            "headingHinglish": "Part F: Teeno niyam ek saath",
            "body": "| Niyam | Kehta kya hai | Kab dikhta hai |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pehla (ellipse) | Orbit ellipse hai, Sooraj ek focus par | Sooraj se doori badalti hai |\n| Doosra (barabar area) | Barabar time mein barabar area | Perihelion par tez, aphelion par dheere |\n| Teesra (T² ∝ r³) | Door wale planet ka chakkar lamba, T² ÷ r³ ek hi number | Mars, Jupiter, Saturn... sab par |\n\nYe teeno niyam **kyun** sach hain, iska jawab Newton ke gravity ke universal law se milta hai. Pehle do niyamon ki poori wajah aage ki class mein aayegi, isliye yahan unhe derive nahi karte. Teesre niyam ka seedha hisaab Topic 4 mein satellite ke orbit ke formula se aayega.",
            "bodyHinglish": "| Niyam | Kehta kya hai | Kab dikhta hai |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pehla (ellipse) | Orbit ellipse hai, Sooraj ek focus par | Sooraj se doori badalti hai |\n| Doosra (barabar area) | Barabar time mein barabar area | Perihelion par tez, aphelion par dheere |\n| Teesra (T² ∝ r³) | Door wale planet ka chakkar lamba, T² ÷ r³ ek hi number | Mars, Jupiter, Saturn... sab par |\n\nYe teeno niyam **kyun** sach hain, iska jawab Newton ke gravity ke universal law se milta hai. Pehle do niyamon ki poori wajah aage ki class mein aayegi, isliye yahan unhe derive nahi karte. Teesre niyam ka seedha hisaab Topic 4 mein satellite ke orbit ke formula se aayega."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "body": "**Galti 1: \"Sooraj ellipse ke centre par hota hai.\"**\n\nYe galat hai: Sooraj **ek focus par** hai. Demo: Earth ke liye perihelion lagbhag 147.1 million km aur aphelion lagbhag 152.1 million km hai. Agar Sooraj ellipse ke centre par hota to dono doori **barabar** hoti. Farak 152.1 − 147.1 = 5.0 million km hai, yaani Sooraj centre se (5.0 ÷ 2 =) 2.5 million km hat kar hai. Ek aur demo: dhaage wale ellipse mein agar dono pin ko ek hi jagah milaa do (focus ek ho jaaye, matlab centre par) to ghera **circle** ban jaata hai (Mrunmayee ka \"ek pin\" wala jawab). Asli ellipse tabhi banta hai jab pin ke beech doori ho. Earth ka ellipse itna kam dabaa hai ki aankh se circle lagta hai, isliye ye galti aasani se ho jaati hai.\n\n**Galti 2: \"Planet hamesha ek jaisi speed se chalta hai.\"**\n\nGalat. Doosre niyam se planet perihelion ke paas **tez** aur aphelion ke paas **dheere** chalta hai. Demo: Example 4 mein kalpit planet Z ki speed 20 km/s (perihelion) aur 5 km/s (aphelion) thi: 4 guna ka farak. Example 5 mein Earth ki speed 30.3 km/s se 29.3 km/s tak badalti hai. Agar speed hamesha ek hi rehti, to chhote radius wala wedge utne hi time mein chhota area jhaadta, jo \"barabar time mein barabar area\" ke khilaaf hota.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Galti 1: \"Sooraj ellipse ke centre par hota hai.\"**\n\nYe galat hai: Sooraj **ek focus par** hai. Demo: Earth ke liye perihelion lagbhag 147.1 million km aur aphelion lagbhag 152.1 million km hai. Agar Sooraj ellipse ke centre par hota to dono doori **barabar** hoti. Farak 152.1 − 147.1 = 5.0 million km hai, yaani Sooraj centre se (5.0 ÷ 2 =) 2.5 million km hat kar hai. Ek aur demo: dhaage wale ellipse mein agar dono pin ko ek hi jagah milaa do (focus ek ho jaaye, matlab centre par) to ghera **circle** ban jaata hai (Mrunmayee ka \"ek pin\" wala jawab). Asli ellipse tabhi banta hai jab pin ke beech doori ho. Earth ka ellipse itna kam dabaa hai ki aankh se circle lagta hai, isliye ye galti aasani se ho jaati hai.\n\n**Galti 2: \"Planet hamesha ek jaisi speed se chalta hai.\"**\n\nGalat. Doosre niyam se planet perihelion ke paas **tez** aur aphelion ke paas **dheere** chalta hai. Demo: Example 4 mein kalpit planet Z ki speed 20 km/s (perihelion) aur 5 km/s (aphelion) thi: 4 guna ka farak. Example 5 mein Earth ki speed 30.3 km/s se 29.3 km/s tak badalti hai. Agar speed hamesha ek hi rehti, to chhote radius wala wedge utne hi time mein chhota area jhaadta, jo \"barabar time mein barabar area\" ke khilaaf hota."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- **Tycho Brahe** ne saalon tak planets (khaaskar Mars) ki jagah bina telescope ke naapi. **Kepler** ne is data se teen niyam nikaale: 1609 mein do aur 1619 mein ek. Baad mein Newton ne wajah (gravity) batayi.\n- **Pehla niyam:** planet ka orbit **ellipse** hai, **Sooraj ek focus par** (centre par nahi). Ellipse ke har point se dono focus ki doori ka jod ek jaisa. Dhaage aur do pin se ellipse banta hai.\n- **Eccentricity e = c ÷ a:** e = 0 circle, e jitna bada utna dabaa. Perihelion = a (1 − e), aphelion = a (1 + e). Earth ka e lagbhag 0.017.\n- **Doosra niyam:** barabar time mein barabar area. Perihelion par planet tez, aphelion par dheere. Do points par r_peri × v_peri = r_aph × v_aph.\n- **Teesra niyam:** T² ∝ r³, yaani T₁² ÷ T₂² = r₁³ ÷ r₂³ (r = a, average doori). T saal aur r AU mein ho to Earth ke liye T² ÷ r³ = 1.\n- Kepler ke niyam **satellite aur Chaand par bhi** chalte hain (Earth ek focus par), par constant alag.",
            "bodyHinglish": "- **Tycho Brahe** ne saalon tak planets (khaaskar Mars) ki jagah bina telescope ke naapi. **Kepler** ne is data se teen niyam nikaale: 1609 mein do aur 1619 mein ek. Baad mein Newton ne wajah (gravity) batayi.\n- **Pehla niyam:** planet ka orbit **ellipse** hai, **Sooraj ek focus par** (centre par nahi). Ellipse ke har point se dono focus ki doori ka jod ek jaisa. Dhaage aur do pin se ellipse banta hai.\n- **Eccentricity e = c ÷ a:** e = 0 circle, e jitna bada utna dabaa. Perihelion = a (1 − e), aphelion = a (1 + e). Earth ka e lagbhag 0.017.\n- **Doosra niyam:** barabar time mein barabar area. Perihelion par planet tez, aphelion par dheere. Do points par r_peri × v_peri = r_aph × v_aph.\n- **Teesra niyam:** T² ∝ r³, yaani T₁² ÷ T₂² = r₁³ ÷ r₂³ (r = a, average doori). T saal aur r AU mein ho to Earth ke liye T² ÷ r³ = 1.\n- Kepler ke niyam **satellite aur Chaand par bhi** chalte hain (Earth ek focus par), par constant alag."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. Mars is at a mean distance of 1.52 AU from the Sun. Find its time period in years using Kepler's third law.\n\n2. Jupiter takes 11.9 years to go around the Sun. Find its mean distance from the Sun in AU and in metres (1 AU = 1.5 × 10¹¹ m).\n\n3. Neptune takes 165 years for one orbit. Find its mean distance from the Sun in AU.\n\n4. Venus is 0.72 AU from the Sun. Find its time period in days (take 1 year = 365 days).\n\n5. Jupiter is 5.20 AU from the Sun and takes 11.9 years for one orbit. Using the ratio method, find the time period of Saturn, which is 9.58 AU from the Sun.\n\n6. Neptune has r = 30.1 AU and T = 165 years. Using these, find the time period of Uranus, which is at 19.2 AU.\n\n7. Mars is nearest to the Sun at 1.38 AU and farthest at 1.67 AU. (a) Find its mean distance a. (b) Use Kepler's third law to find its time period. (c) Find its eccentricity. (d) If its speed at aphelion is 22.0 km/s, find its speed at perihelion.\n\n8. Halley's comet takes about 76 years to go around the Sun and its closest distance to the Sun is 0.59 AU. (a) Find the mean distance a of its orbit in AU. (b) Find its farthest distance from the Sun. (c) If its speed at the closest point is 54 km/s, find its speed at the farthest point.\n\n9. The Moon is at 3.84 × 10⁸ m from the Earth and takes 27.3 days for one orbit. (a) A satellite orbits the Earth at 1.92 × 10⁸ m. Find its time period in days. (b) Another satellite has a time period of 2.0 days. Find the radius of its orbit.\n\n10. A thread loop of length 30 cm is put around two pins 10 cm apart, and an ellipse is drawn as in the activity. Find r1 + r2, a, c, e and b of the ellipse.\n\n11. Find the average orbital speeds of Venus (r = 0.72 AU, T = 0.62 years) and Saturn (r = 9.58 AU, T = 29.5 years) using speed = 2πr ÷ T, in AU per year and then in km/s (1 AU = 1.5 × 10¹¹ m, 1 year = 3.15 × 10⁷ s). Which planet moves faster, and what does it tell you about planets nearer the Sun?\n\n12. State True or False and give a reason for each:\n   (a) The Sun is at the centre of a planet's elliptical orbit.\n   (b) A planet moves fastest when it is closest to the Sun.\n   (c) T² ÷ r³ is nearly the same for Mars and Jupiter (T in years, r in AU).\n   (d) Kepler's laws are true only for the planets of our Solar System.\n   (e) \"Equal areas in equal times\" means a planet covers equal distances in equal times.\n\n13. Explain in your own words, using the thread-and-two-pins activity, what a focus of an ellipse is and why the shape becomes a circle if both pins are at the same point.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. T² = r³ = 1.52³ = 3.512, to T = √3.512 = **1.87 saal** (lagbhag).\n   Jaanch: 1.874² = 3.512 = 1.52³ ✅\n\n2. r³ = T² = 11.9² = 141.6, to r = ∛141.6 = **5.21 AU**. Metres mein: 5.21 × 1.5 × 10¹¹ m = **7.8 × 10¹¹ m**.\n   Jaanch: 5.21³ = 141.4 ≈ 141.6 ✅ (rounding)\n\n3. r³ = T² = 165² = 27,225, to r = ∛27,225 = **30.1 AU** (lagbhag; 30.08).\n   Jaanch: 30.1³ = 27,271 ≈ 27,225 ✅\n\n4. T² = 0.72³ = 0.3732, to T = **0.611 saal**. Dinon mein: 0.611 × 365 = **223 din** (lagbhag).\n   Jaanch: 0.611² = 0.373 ✅\n\n5. T_S ÷ 11.9 = (9.58 ÷ 5.20)^(3/2) = (1.842)^1.5 = 2.50. T_S = 11.9 × 2.50 = **29.8 saal** (lagbhag).\n   Jaanch: (29.8 ÷ 11.9)² = 6.27 aur (1.842)³ = 6.25 ✅. Diye gaye table mein 29.5 saal hai: ~1 percent ka farak data ke rounding ka hai.\n\n6. T_U ÷ 165 = (19.2 ÷ 30.1)^(3/2) = (0.638)^1.5 = 0.5095. T_U = 165 × 0.5095 = **84.1 saal** (lagbhag).\n   Jaanch: (84.1 ÷ 165)² = 0.260 aur (0.638)³ = 0.260 ✅\n\n7. (a) a = (1.38 + 1.67) ÷ 2 = **1.525 AU**.\n   (b) T² = a³ = 1.525³ = 3.546, to T = **1.88 saal** (lagbhag).\n   (c) c = (1.67 − 1.38) ÷ 2 = 0.145 AU, to e = c ÷ a = 0.145 ÷ 1.525 = **0.095** (lagbhag).\n   (d) v_peri = v_aph × (r_aph ÷ r_peri) = 22.0 km/s × (1.67 ÷ 1.38) = **26.6 km/s** (lagbhag).\n   Jaanch: 1.38 × 26.6 = 36.7 aur 1.67 × 22.0 = 36.7 ✅; a (1 − e) = 1.525 × 0.905 = 1.38 ✅.\n\n8. (a) T² = a³, to a = ∛(76²) = ∛5776 = **17.9 AU** (lagbhag).\n   (b) Perihelion + aphelion = 2a, to aphelion = 2 × 17.94 − 0.59 = **35.3 AU** (lagbhag).\n   (c) v_far = 54 km/s × (0.59 ÷ 35.3) = **0.90 km/s** (lagbhag).\n   Jaanch: 0.59 × 54 = 31.9 aur 35.3 × 0.90 = 31.8 ✅. Ye comet perihelion par bahut tez aur aphelion par bahut dheere chalta hai: ye bahut dabe hue ellipse ki nishani hai (e lagbhag 0.97).\n\n9. (a) T ÷ 27.3 = (1.92 × 10⁸ ÷ 3.84 × 10⁸)^(3/2) = (0.5)^1.5 = 0.3536, to T = **9.65 din** (lagbhag).\n   (b) r ÷ 3.84 × 10⁸ = (2.0 ÷ 27.3)^(2/3) = (0.07326)^(2/3) = 0.175, to r = 3.84 × 10⁸ × 0.175 = **6.7 × 10⁷ m** (lagbhag).\n   Jaanch: (9.65 ÷ 27.3)² = 0.125 = (0.5)³ ✅; (2.0 ÷ 27.3)² = 0.00537 aur 0.175³ = 0.00536 ✅.\n\n10. r1 + r2 = L − d = 30 − 10 = **20 cm**. 2a = 20 cm, to **a = 10 cm**. c = d ÷ 2 = **5 cm**. e = c ÷ a = 5 ÷ 10 = **0.5**. b = √(a² − c²) = √(100 − 25) = √75 = **8.66 cm** (lagbhag).\n    Jaanch: a (1 − e) = 5 cm aur a (1 + e) = 15 cm, jod 20 cm = r1 + r2 ✅; 5² + 8.66² = 25 + 75 = 100 = 10² ✅.\n\n11. Venus: 2π × 0.72 ÷ 0.62 = 7.30 AU/saal. Km/s mein: 7.30 × 1.5 × 10¹¹ m ÷ 3.15 × 10⁷ s = 3.47 × 10⁴ m/s = **34.7 km/s**.\n    Saturn: 2π × 9.58 ÷ 29.5 = 2.04 AU/saal. Km/s mein: 2.04 × 1.5 × 10¹¹ ÷ 3.15 × 10⁷ = 9.7 × 10³ m/s = **9.7 km/s**.\n    **Venus tez** hai: Sooraj ke paas wale planet tez ghoomte hain aur door wale dheere. Ye Class 9 ke \"jitna paas, utna tez\" pattern ka hisaab hai.\n    Jaanch: 34.7 ÷ 9.7 = 3.6, aur Saturn ki doori ÷ Venus ki doori = 13.3: speed doori ki tulna mein bahut kam badli, jaisa teesre niyam se hona chahiye ✅.\n\n12. (a) **False** ❌. Sooraj ek focus par hai, centre par nahi.\n    (b) **True** ✅. Perihelion par doori kam, to speed zyada (doosra niyam).\n    (c) **True** ✅. Dono ke liye T² ÷ r³ lagbhag 1 hai (table dekho: 1.01 aur 1.01).\n    (d) **False** ❌. Satellite, Chaand aur kisi bhi cheez ke liye jo kisi bade pind ke chaaro taraf gravity se ghoomti hai, niyam sach hain.\n    (e) **False** ❌. Barabar time mein **area** barabar hota hai, doori nahi: perihelion ke paas arc lamba aur aphelion ke paas chhota hota hai.\n\n13. Focus wo khaas point hai jahan pin lagi hai: ellipse ke har point se dono focus tak ki doori ka jod ek jaisa rehta hai (dhaage ki lambai se). Agar dono pin ek hi point par hon to r1 = r2 = hamesha ek hi doori ban jaati hai (dhaage ka aadha hissa), yaani pencil ek hi point se hamesha barabar doori par rehta hai, aur wahi circle ki paribhasha hai. Isliye e = 0 aur ellipse circle ban jaata hai.\n\n---\n\nAaj Mrunmayee ne seekha ki planet ka raasta ellipse hai, Sooraj uske ek focus par baitha hai, planet paas aate hi tez ho jaata hai, aur door ke planet ka saal lamba hota hai: dhaage aur do pin se saare niyam dikh gaye. Ab jab bhi tum ek ande jaisa ghera dekho, to yaad aayega ki aasmaan mein ek planet bhi isi tarah chal raha hai.\n\n**Aage kya?** Gravity Earth ki sataah par g = 9.8 m/s² deti hai, par Topic 2 mein dekhenge ki jab hum bahut upar ya zameen ke andar jaate hain, ya Moon aur doosre grahon par pahunchte hain to g kitna badal jaata hai.",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. Mars is at a mean distance of 1.52 AU from the Sun. Find its time period in years using Kepler's third law.\n\n2. Jupiter takes 11.9 years to go around the Sun. Find its mean distance from the Sun in AU and in metres (1 AU = 1.5 × 10¹¹ m).\n\n3. Neptune takes 165 years for one orbit. Find its mean distance from the Sun in AU.\n\n4. Venus is 0.72 AU from the Sun. Find its time period in days (take 1 year = 365 days).\n\n5. Jupiter is 5.20 AU from the Sun and takes 11.9 years for one orbit. Using the ratio method, find the time period of Saturn, which is 9.58 AU from the Sun.\n\n6. Neptune has r = 30.1 AU and T = 165 years. Using these, find the time period of Uranus, which is at 19.2 AU.\n\n7. Mars is nearest to the Sun at 1.38 AU and farthest at 1.67 AU. (a) Find its mean distance a. (b) Use Kepler's third law to find its time period. (c) Find its eccentricity. (d) If its speed at aphelion is 22.0 km/s, find its speed at perihelion.\n\n8. Halley's comet takes about 76 years to go around the Sun and its closest distance to the Sun is 0.59 AU. (a) Find the mean distance a of its orbit in AU. (b) Find its farthest distance from the Sun. (c) If its speed at the closest point is 54 km/s, find its speed at the farthest point.\n\n9. The Moon is at 3.84 × 10⁸ m from the Earth and takes 27.3 days for one orbit. (a) A satellite orbits the Earth at 1.92 × 10⁸ m. Find its time period in days. (b) Another satellite has a time period of 2.0 days. Find the radius of its orbit.\n\n10. A thread loop of length 30 cm is put around two pins 10 cm apart, and an ellipse is drawn as in the activity. Find r1 + r2, a, c, e and b of the ellipse.\n\n11. Find the average orbital speeds of Venus (r = 0.72 AU, T = 0.62 years) and Saturn (r = 9.58 AU, T = 29.5 years) using speed = 2πr ÷ T, in AU per year and then in km/s (1 AU = 1.5 × 10¹¹ m, 1 year = 3.15 × 10⁷ s). Which planet moves faster, and what does it tell you about planets nearer the Sun?\n\n12. State True or False and give a reason for each:\n   (a) The Sun is at the centre of a planet's elliptical orbit.\n   (b) A planet moves fastest when it is closest to the Sun.\n   (c) T² ÷ r³ is nearly the same for Mars and Jupiter (T in years, r in AU).\n   (d) Kepler's laws are true only for the planets of our Solar System.\n   (e) \"Equal areas in equal times\" means a planet covers equal distances in equal times.\n\n13. Explain in your own words, using the thread-and-two-pins activity, what a focus of an ellipse is and why the shape becomes a circle if both pins are at the same point.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. T² = r³ = 1.52³ = 3.512, to T = √3.512 = **1.87 saal** (lagbhag).\n   Jaanch: 1.874² = 3.512 = 1.52³ ✅\n\n2. r³ = T² = 11.9² = 141.6, to r = ∛141.6 = **5.21 AU**. Metres mein: 5.21 × 1.5 × 10¹¹ m = **7.8 × 10¹¹ m**.\n   Jaanch: 5.21³ = 141.4 ≈ 141.6 ✅ (rounding)\n\n3. r³ = T² = 165² = 27,225, to r = ∛27,225 = **30.1 AU** (lagbhag; 30.08).\n   Jaanch: 30.1³ = 27,271 ≈ 27,225 ✅\n\n4. T² = 0.72³ = 0.3732, to T = **0.611 saal**. Dinon mein: 0.611 × 365 = **223 din** (lagbhag).\n   Jaanch: 0.611² = 0.373 ✅\n\n5. T_S ÷ 11.9 = (9.58 ÷ 5.20)^(3/2) = (1.842)^1.5 = 2.50. T_S = 11.9 × 2.50 = **29.8 saal** (lagbhag).\n   Jaanch: (29.8 ÷ 11.9)² = 6.27 aur (1.842)³ = 6.25 ✅. Diye gaye table mein 29.5 saal hai: ~1 percent ka farak data ke rounding ka hai.\n\n6. T_U ÷ 165 = (19.2 ÷ 30.1)^(3/2) = (0.638)^1.5 = 0.5095. T_U = 165 × 0.5095 = **84.1 saal** (lagbhag).\n   Jaanch: (84.1 ÷ 165)² = 0.260 aur (0.638)³ = 0.260 ✅\n\n7. (a) a = (1.38 + 1.67) ÷ 2 = **1.525 AU**.\n   (b) T² = a³ = 1.525³ = 3.546, to T = **1.88 saal** (lagbhag).\n   (c) c = (1.67 − 1.38) ÷ 2 = 0.145 AU, to e = c ÷ a = 0.145 ÷ 1.525 = **0.095** (lagbhag).\n   (d) v_peri = v_aph × (r_aph ÷ r_peri) = 22.0 km/s × (1.67 ÷ 1.38) = **26.6 km/s** (lagbhag).\n   Jaanch: 1.38 × 26.6 = 36.7 aur 1.67 × 22.0 = 36.7 ✅; a (1 − e) = 1.525 × 0.905 = 1.38 ✅.\n\n8. (a) T² = a³, to a = ∛(76²) = ∛5776 = **17.9 AU** (lagbhag).\n   (b) Perihelion + aphelion = 2a, to aphelion = 2 × 17.94 − 0.59 = **35.3 AU** (lagbhag).\n   (c) v_far = 54 km/s × (0.59 ÷ 35.3) = **0.90 km/s** (lagbhag).\n   Jaanch: 0.59 × 54 = 31.9 aur 35.3 × 0.90 = 31.8 ✅. Ye comet perihelion par bahut tez aur aphelion par bahut dheere chalta hai: ye bahut dabe hue ellipse ki nishani hai (e lagbhag 0.97).\n\n9. (a) T ÷ 27.3 = (1.92 × 10⁸ ÷ 3.84 × 10⁸)^(3/2) = (0.5)^1.5 = 0.3536, to T = **9.65 din** (lagbhag).\n   (b) r ÷ 3.84 × 10⁸ = (2.0 ÷ 27.3)^(2/3) = (0.07326)^(2/3) = 0.175, to r = 3.84 × 10⁸ × 0.175 = **6.7 × 10⁷ m** (lagbhag).\n   Jaanch: (9.65 ÷ 27.3)² = 0.125 = (0.5)³ ✅; (2.0 ÷ 27.3)² = 0.00537 aur 0.175³ = 0.00536 ✅.\n\n10. r1 + r2 = L − d = 30 − 10 = **20 cm**. 2a = 20 cm, to **a = 10 cm**. c = d ÷ 2 = **5 cm**. e = c ÷ a = 5 ÷ 10 = **0.5**. b = √(a² − c²) = √(100 − 25) = √75 = **8.66 cm** (lagbhag).\n    Jaanch: a (1 − e) = 5 cm aur a (1 + e) = 15 cm, jod 20 cm = r1 + r2 ✅; 5² + 8.66² = 25 + 75 = 100 = 10² ✅.\n\n11. Venus: 2π × 0.72 ÷ 0.62 = 7.30 AU/saal. Km/s mein: 7.30 × 1.5 × 10¹¹ m ÷ 3.15 × 10⁷ s = 3.47 × 10⁴ m/s = **34.7 km/s**.\n    Saturn: 2π × 9.58 ÷ 29.5 = 2.04 AU/saal. Km/s mein: 2.04 × 1.5 × 10¹¹ ÷ 3.15 × 10⁷ = 9.7 × 10³ m/s = **9.7 km/s**.\n    **Venus tez** hai: Sooraj ke paas wale planet tez ghoomte hain aur door wale dheere. Ye Class 9 ke \"jitna paas, utna tez\" pattern ka hisaab hai.\n    Jaanch: 34.7 ÷ 9.7 = 3.6, aur Saturn ki doori ÷ Venus ki doori = 13.3: speed doori ki tulna mein bahut kam badli, jaisa teesre niyam se hona chahiye ✅.\n\n12. (a) **False** ❌. Sooraj ek focus par hai, centre par nahi.\n    (b) **True** ✅. Perihelion par doori kam, to speed zyada (doosra niyam).\n    (c) **True** ✅. Dono ke liye T² ÷ r³ lagbhag 1 hai (table dekho: 1.01 aur 1.01).\n    (d) **False** ❌. Satellite, Chaand aur kisi bhi cheez ke liye jo kisi bade pind ke chaaro taraf gravity se ghoomti hai, niyam sach hain.\n    (e) **False** ❌. Barabar time mein **area** barabar hota hai, doori nahi: perihelion ke paas arc lamba aur aphelion ke paas chhota hota hai.\n\n13. Focus wo khaas point hai jahan pin lagi hai: ellipse ke har point se dono focus tak ki doori ka jod ek jaisa rehta hai (dhaage ki lambai se). Agar dono pin ek hi point par hon to r1 = r2 = hamesha ek hi doori ban jaati hai (dhaage ka aadha hissa), yaani pencil ek hi point se hamesha barabar doori par rehta hai, aur wahi circle ki paribhasha hai. Isliye e = 0 aur ellipse circle ban jaata hai.\n\n---\n\nAaj Mrunmayee ne seekha ki planet ka raasta ellipse hai, Sooraj uske ek focus par baitha hai, planet paas aate hi tez ho jaata hai, aur door ke planet ka saal lamba hota hai: dhaage aur do pin se saare niyam dikh gaye. Ab jab bhi tum ek ande jaisa ghera dekho, to yaad aayega ki aasmaan mein ek planet bhi isi tarah chal raha hai.\n\n**Aage kya?** Gravity Earth ki sataah par g = 9.8 m/s² deti hai, par Topic 2 mein dekhenge ki jab hum bahut upar ya zameen ke andar jaate hain, ya Moon aur doosre grahon par pahunchte hain to g kitna badal jaata hai."
          }
