          {
            "heading": "Part F: Kahani ka jawab: ISS par g kitna?",
            "headingHinglish": "Part F: Kahani ka jawab: ISS par g kitna?",
            "body": "Yuvraj ke sawaal par aate hain: kya ISS mein g zero hai? Surface se h = 400 km, R = 6400 km.\n\n**Given:** g = 9.8 m/s², R = 6400 km, h = 400 km\n**Find:** g_h\n**Formula:** g_h = g × (R / (R + h))²\n**Substitution:** R + h = 6800 km. R/(R + h) = 6400 ÷ 6800 = 0.9412. Square = 0.8858. g_h = 9.8 m/s² × 0.8858 = **8.68 m/s²**\n**Answer:** lagbhag **8.7 m/s²**, surface ke g ka lagbhag 89%. Yaani g sirf lagbhag 11% kam hua.\n**Jaanch:** Doori 6400 se 6800 (sirf 1.06 guna) hui, to g 1/1.06² = 0.886 guna hona chahiye ✅. Ye wahi 8.68 m/s² hai jo Class 9 mein ek satellite ke orbit ke liye mila tha.\n\nTo Yuvraj ke saamne ka sach ye hai: ISS par gravity **lagbhag poori** hai, aur wahi gravity ISS ko aur uske andar ke astronaut ko Earth ki taraf kheench rahi hai. Wo astronaut isliye tairte hue dikhte hain ki wo aur ISS **dono saath free fall** mein hain. Ye hum Topic 4 mein poori tarah dekhenge. Abhi itna yaad rakho: **tairna g zero hone ka saboot nahi hai.**",
            "bodyHinglish": "Yuvraj ke sawaal par aate hain: kya ISS mein g zero hai? Surface se h = 400 km, R = 6400 km.\n\n**Given:** g = 9.8 m/s², R = 6400 km, h = 400 km\n**Find:** g_h\n**Formula:** g_h = g × (R / (R + h))²\n**Substitution:** R + h = 6800 km. R/(R + h) = 6400 ÷ 6800 = 0.9412. Square = 0.8858. g_h = 9.8 m/s² × 0.8858 = **8.68 m/s²**\n**Answer:** lagbhag **8.7 m/s²**, surface ke g ka lagbhag 89%. Yaani g sirf lagbhag 11% kam hua.\n**Jaanch:** Doori 6400 se 6800 (sirf 1.06 guna) hui, to g 1/1.06² = 0.886 guna hona chahiye ✅. Ye wahi 8.68 m/s² hai jo Class 9 mein ek satellite ke orbit ke liye mila tha.\n\nTo Yuvraj ke saamne ka sach ye hai: ISS par gravity **lagbhag poori** hai, aur wahi gravity ISS ko aur uske andar ke astronaut ko Earth ki taraf kheench rahi hai. Wo astronaut isliye tairte hue dikhte hain ki wo aur ISS **dono saath free fall** mein hain. Ye hum Topic 4 mein poori tarah dekhenge. Abhi itna yaad rakho: **tairna g zero hone ka saboot nahi hai.**"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "body": "**Galti 1: \"400 km oopar g zero ho jaata hai (isliye astronaut floating).\"**\n\nYe sach lagta hai kyunki video mein sab kuch hawa mein tair raha hai. Par ek chhoti kahani se dekho. Yuvraj ne kaha, \"Agar wahan g zero hota, to ISS ko Earth kheench hi nahi rahi hoti, aur wo seedha door ud jaata.\" Mr. Godbole ne poochha, \"To phir ISS Earth ke gird chakkar kyun lagata hai? Gol ghoomne ke liye bhi to koi **kheench** chahiye.\" Yuvraj ruk gaya.\n\nNumber ne baat saaf kar di (Part F): 400 km par g = 8.7 m/s², surface ke g ke lagbhag 89%. Aur formula g_h = g (R/(R + h))² mein R/(R + h) kabhi zero nahi hota, chahe h kitna bhi bada ho: g **kam** hota jaata hai, par kisi bhi height par exact zero nahi hota. Surface se bahut door, jaise Earth ke chaaron taraf ke aasmaan mein dur-dur tak, bhi gravity ka kuch na kuch asar rehta hai.\n\n**Niyam:** g oopar jaane par **kam hota hai par kabhi zero nahi.** Astronaut ka tairna, g zero hone se nahi, **free fall** se hai (Topic 4).\n\n**Galti 2: \"Zameen ke andar jaane par g badhta hai, kyunki centre paas aa raha hai.\"**\n\nYe dimag ko sahi lagta hai: gravity centre ki taraf kheenchti hai, to paas jaane par zyada honi chahiye (jaise 1/r² mein r chhota). Ek demonstration se dekho: maan lo Earth ke andar d = 4800 km gehra gaye, yaani centre se sirf r = 1600 km door. 1/r² wale rule se ye soch kehti hai: R/r = 6400 ÷ 1600 = 4, to g = 9.8 × 16 = **156.8 m/s²**. Par ye galat hai, kyunki hamare **bahar ki parat** ab hum par net force nahi daalti (Part C). Sahi rule: g_d = 9.8 × (1 − 0.75) = **2.45 m/s²**, jo surface ke g se bahut kam hai.\n\nIsse bhi aasan test: Earth ke bilkul centre par chaaron taraf ka kheench barabar hai: kisi taraf se kheenchne ka koi **fayda** nahi, to net force zero, g = 0. Agar \"paas jaane par g badhta hai\" sach hota to centre par to infinite g hona chahiye tha, jo asambhav hai.\n\n**Niyam:** Surface ke **oopar** g ghat'ta hai (1/r² se), surface ke **neeche** bhi ghat'ta hai (seedhi line mein, g (1 − d/R) se), aur centre par zero hai. Surface par sabse zyada hai (simple model mein; asli Earth ke bhaari core ki wajah se kuch hazaar km tak g thoda badhta hai, par \"centre ki taraf g badhta hi jaata hai\" galat hai: centre par g zero hai).",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Galti 1: \"400 km oopar g zero ho jaata hai (isliye astronaut floating).\"**\n\nYe sach lagta hai kyunki video mein sab kuch hawa mein tair raha hai. Par ek chhoti kahani se dekho. Yuvraj ne kaha, \"Agar wahan g zero hota, to ISS ko Earth kheench hi nahi rahi hoti, aur wo seedha door ud jaata.\" Mr. Godbole ne poochha, \"To phir ISS Earth ke gird chakkar kyun lagata hai? Gol ghoomne ke liye bhi to koi **kheench** chahiye.\" Yuvraj ruk gaya.\n\nNumber ne baat saaf kar di (Part F): 400 km par g = 8.7 m/s², surface ke g ke lagbhag 89%. Aur formula g_h = g (R/(R + h))² mein R/(R + h) kabhi zero nahi hota, chahe h kitna bhi bada ho: g **kam** hota jaata hai, par kisi bhi height par exact zero nahi hota. Surface se bahut door, jaise Earth ke chaaron taraf ke aasmaan mein dur-dur tak, bhi gravity ka kuch na kuch asar rehta hai.\n\n**Niyam:** g oopar jaane par **kam hota hai par kabhi zero nahi.** Astronaut ka tairna, g zero hone se nahi, **free fall** se hai (Topic 4).\n\n**Galti 2: \"Zameen ke andar jaane par g badhta hai, kyunki centre paas aa raha hai.\"**\n\nYe dimag ko sahi lagta hai: gravity centre ki taraf kheenchti hai, to paas jaane par zyada honi chahiye (jaise 1/r² mein r chhota). Ek demonstration se dekho: maan lo Earth ke andar d = 4800 km gehra gaye, yaani centre se sirf r = 1600 km door. 1/r² wale rule se ye soch kehti hai: R/r = 6400 ÷ 1600 = 4, to g = 9.8 × 16 = **156.8 m/s²**. Par ye galat hai, kyunki hamare **bahar ki parat** ab hum par net force nahi daalti (Part C). Sahi rule: g_d = 9.8 × (1 − 0.75) = **2.45 m/s²**, jo surface ke g se bahut kam hai.\n\nIsse bhi aasan test: Earth ke bilkul centre par chaaron taraf ka kheench barabar hai: kisi taraf se kheenchne ka koi **fayda** nahi, to net force zero, g = 0. Agar \"paas jaane par g badhta hai\" sach hota to centre par to infinite g hona chahiye tha, jo asambhav hai.\n\n**Niyam:** Surface ke **oopar** g ghat'ta hai (1/r² se), surface ke **neeche** bhi ghat'ta hai (seedhi line mein, g (1 − d/R) se), aur centre par zero hai. Surface par sabse zyada hai (simple model mein; asli Earth ke bhaari core ki wajah se kuch hazaar km tak g thoda badhta hai, par \"centre ki taraf g badhta hi jaata hai\" galat hai: centre par g zero hai)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- **Recall:** g = GM/R² (Class 9). Doori **Earth ke centre se** naapte hain: surface se h oopar ho to r = R + h.\n- **Height par:** g_h = g × (R / (R + h))². h badhne par g ghat'ta hai, par zero kabhi nahi hota. 400 km par g lagbhag 8.7 m/s² (surface ka lagbhag 89%).\n- **Depth par (simple model, barabar density):** g_d = g (1 − d/R). Seedhi line mein ghat'ta hai, centre par 0. Asli Earth mein andar ka core isse thoda alag asar deta hai.\n- **Poles aur equator:** g_pole ≈ 9.83 m/s², g_equator ≈ 9.78 m/s². Wajah: Earth ka ubhaar aur uska ghoomna. Mass nahi badalta.\n- **Doosre grah:** g_Moon ≈ 1.6, g_Mars ≈ 3.7, g_Jupiter ≈ 25 m/s². Weight W = m g badalta hai, mass nahi.\n- **Astronaut ka hop:** wahi takeoff speed, to oonchai g ke ulte anupaat mein; Chaand par lagbhag 6 guna.\n- **Ratio vidhi:** g_naya = g × (mass ka guna) ÷ (radius ke guna ka square).\n- **Galtiyan:** \"400 km par g zero\" galat (8.7 m/s² hai). \"Depth par g badhta hai\" galat (ghat'ta hai, centre par 0).",
            "bodyHinglish": "- **Recall:** g = GM/R² (Class 9). Doori **Earth ke centre se** naapte hain: surface se h oopar ho to r = R + h.\n- **Height par:** g_h = g × (R / (R + h))². h badhne par g ghat'ta hai, par zero kabhi nahi hota. 400 km par g lagbhag 8.7 m/s² (surface ka lagbhag 89%).\n- **Depth par (simple model, barabar density):** g_d = g (1 − d/R). Seedhi line mein ghat'ta hai, centre par 0. Asli Earth mein andar ka core isse thoda alag asar deta hai.\n- **Poles aur equator:** g_pole ≈ 9.83 m/s², g_equator ≈ 9.78 m/s². Wajah: Earth ka ubhaar aur uska ghoomna. Mass nahi badalta.\n- **Doosre grah:** g_Moon ≈ 1.6, g_Mars ≈ 3.7, g_Jupiter ≈ 25 m/s². Weight W = m g badalta hai, mass nahi.\n- **Astronaut ka hop:** wahi takeoff speed, to oonchai g ke ulte anupaat mein; Chaand par lagbhag 6 guna.\n- **Ratio vidhi:** g_naya = g × (mass ka guna) ÷ (radius ke guna ka square).\n- **Galtiyan:** \"400 km par g zero\" galat (8.7 m/s² hai). \"Depth par g badhta hai\" galat (ghat'ta hai, centre par 0)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. Find the acceleration due to gravity at a height of 100 km above the Earth's surface. (Use g = 9.8 m/s² at the surface and R = 6400 km.)\n\n2. A communication satellite is at a height of 36,000 km above the Earth's surface. Find g at that height, and say what fraction of the surface value it is. (g = 9.8 m/s², R = 6400 km.)\n\n3. A miner works 1 km below the surface of the Earth. Find g there and by how much it differs from the surface value. (g = 9.8 m/s², R = 6400 km. Use the g_d = g (1 − d/R) rule.)\n\n4. Find g at a depth equal to half the Earth's radius (d = R/2), taking g = 9.8 m/s² at the surface.\n\n5. On Mars, g is about 3.7 m/s². A student has a mass of 48 kg. Find her weight on the Earth and on Mars, and her mass on Mars.\n\n6. On Jupiter (at the cloud tops), g is about 25 m/s². Find the weight of a 44 kg student on the Earth and at Jupiter's cloud tops. How many times bigger is it on Jupiter?\n\n7. A mountaineer of mass 76 kg stands on a mountain peak 8 km above sea level. Find g there (take R = 6400 km), and the change in his weight compared with sea level. Comment on whether this change would be noticed.\n\n8. A person jumps 0.40 m high on the Earth. Using the same take-off speed, how high would she jump on Mars (g = 3.7 m/s²)? (Take g_E = 9.8 m/s² and ignore air.)\n\n9. At what height above the Earth's surface does g become half of its surface value? (R = 6400 km. Hint: use g_h / g = (R / (R + h))² = 1/2 and a square root.)\n\n10. At what depth below the surface does g fall to 7.84 m/s²? Take g = 9.8 m/s² and R = 6400 km.\n\n11. A 100 kg person is weighed using a spring balance at the North Pole and at the equator. Find the two readings (g_pole = 9.83 m/s², g_equator = 9.78 m/s²). Will a beam balance show a different mass at these two places?\n\n12. State True or False and correct the false statements:\n    (a) At the height of the ISS (about 400 km), g is zero.\n    (b) Inside the Earth, g increases steadily as we go towards the centre.\n    (c) A 1 kg object has the same mass on the Moon as on the Earth.\n    (d) The weight of an object on Mars is more than on Jupiter.\n    (e) The value of g at the poles is slightly greater than at the equator.\n\n13. Explain in your own words why astronauts hop to a great height on the Moon even though their mass is the same as on the Earth.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. **Given:** h = 100 km. **Formula:** g_h = g (R / (R + h))². **Substitution:** R + h = 6500 km. R/(R + h) = 6400 ÷ 6500 = 0.98462. Square = 0.96945. g_h = 9.8 × 0.96945 = **9.50 m/s²**. **Jaanch:** 9.50 < 9.8 ✅ aur 9.50 ÷ 9.8 = 0.969 ≈ (0.9846)² ✅. 100 km par g sirf lagbhag 3% kam hua.\n\n2. **Given:** h = 36,000 km. **Formula:** g_h = g (R / (R + h))². **Substitution:** R + h = 6400 + 36,000 = 42,400 km. R/(R + h) = 6400 ÷ 42,400 = 0.15094. Square = 0.022783. g_h = 9.8 × 0.022783 = **0.223 m/s²** (lagbhag 0.22). Hissa = 0.022783, yaani lagbhag **2.3%** surface ke g ka. **Jaanch:** Doori 6.625 guna hui (42,400 ÷ 6400), to g 1/6.625² = 0.0228 guna ✅. Ye zero nahi hai: aur Topic 4 mein dekhenge ki isi chhote g se satellite 24 ghante mein ek chakkar lagata hai.\n\n3. **Given:** d = 1 km. **Formula:** g_d = g (1 − d/R). **Substitution:** d/R = 1 ÷ 6400 = 0.00015625. g_d = 9.8 × (1 − 0.00015625) = 9.8 × 0.99984 = **9.7985 m/s²** (lagbhag 9.80). Farak = 9.8 − 9.7985 = **0.0015 m/s²**. **Jaanch:** Farak = g × d/R = 9.8 × 0.00015625 = 0.0015 ✅. Itna chhota farak koi nahi mehsoos kar sakta, isliye khaan mein bhi g = 9.8 maanna theek hai.\n\n4. **Given:** d = R/2. **Formula:** g_d = g (1 − d/R). **Substitution:** d/R = 1/2. g_d = 9.8 × (1 − 0.5) = 9.8 × 0.5 = **4.9 m/s²**. **Jaanch:** 4.9 ÷ 9.8 = 0.5 = 1 − 0.5 ✅. (d = 3200 km, aadhe raaste par aadha g.)\n\n5. **Given:** m = 48 kg, g_E = 9.8 m/s², g_Mars = 3.7 m/s². **Formula:** W = m g. **Substitution:** W_E = 48 × 9.8 = 470.4 N. W_Mars = 48 × 3.7 = 177.6 N. **Answer:** Earth par **470.4 N**, Mars par **177.6 N**, mass Mars par bhi **48 kg**. **Jaanch:** 177.6 ÷ 470.4 = 0.378 = 3.7 ÷ 9.8 ✅.\n\n6. **Given:** m = 44 kg, g_E = 9.8, g_J = 25 m/s². **Formula:** W = m g. **Substitution:** W_E = 44 × 9.8 = 431.2 N. W_J = 44 × 25 = 1100 N. Anupaat = 1100 ÷ 431.2 = 2.55. **Answer:** **431.2 N** aur **1100 N**; Jupiter par lagbhag **2.6 guna**. **Jaanch:** 25 ÷ 9.8 = 2.55 ✅. Mass dono jagah 44 kg hi hai.\n\n7. **Given:** m = 76 kg, h = 8 km. **Formula:** g_h = g (R / (R + h))². **Substitution:** R + h = 6408 km. R/(R + h) = 6400 ÷ 6408 = 0.998752. Square = 0.997505. g_h = 9.8 × 0.997505 = **9.7755 m/s²** (lagbhag 9.78). Sea level par W = 76 × 9.8 = 744.8 N. Peak par W = 76 × 9.7755 = 742.9 N. Farak = **lagbhag 1.9 N**. **Jaanch:** Farak ≈ 2h/R × W = 2 × 8 ÷ 6400 × 744.8 = 1.86 N ✅. Itna chhota farak (lagbhag 0.25%) bina tez instrument ke mehsoos nahi hoga. Yaad rakho: 8 km, 6400 km ke saamne bahut chhota hai.\n\n8. **Given:** h_E = 0.40 m, g_E = 9.8, g_M = 3.7 m/s². **Formula:** u² = 2 g h (wahi u dono jagah), to h ∝ 1/g. **Substitution:** h_Mars = h_E × g_E ÷ g_Mars = 0.40 × 9.8 ÷ 3.7 = 3.92 ÷ 3.7 = **1.06 m**. **Jaanch:** Takeoff speed u = √(2 × 9.8 × 0.40) = √7.84 = 2.8 m/s. Mars par h = u² ÷ (2 × 3.7) = 7.84 ÷ 7.4 = 1.06 m ✅.\n\n9. **Given:** g_h / g = 1/2. **Formula:** (R / (R + h))² = 1/2, to R / (R + h) = 1/√2. **Substitution:** R + h = √2 × R = 1.4142 × 6400 = 9051 km, to h = 9051 − 6400 = **lagbhag 2651 km** (yaani 0.414 R). **Jaanch:** g_h = 9.8 × (6400 ÷ 9051)² = 9.8 × (0.7071)² = 9.8 × 0.5 = 4.9 m/s² ✅.\n\n10. **Given:** g_d = 7.84 m/s², g = 9.8 m/s². **Formula:** g_d = g (1 − d/R), to 1 − d/R = g_d ÷ g. **Substitution:** 7.84 ÷ 9.8 = 0.8, to d/R = 1 − 0.8 = 0.2. d = 0.2 × 6400 = **1280 km**. **Jaanch:** 9.8 × (1 − 0.2) = 9.8 × 0.8 = 7.84 m/s² ✅.\n\n11. **Given:** m = 100 kg, g_pole = 9.83, g_eq = 9.78 m/s². **Formula:** W = m g. **Substitution:** W_pole = 100 × 9.83 = 983 N. W_eq = 100 × 9.78 = 978 N. **Answer:** spring balance **983 N** aur **978 N** (farak 5 N). Beam balance dono jagah **100 kg** hi dikhayegi, kyunki wo apne mass ko doosre known mass se compare karti hai (g dono taraf ek jaisa lagta hai). **Jaanch:** 983 ÷ 100 = 9.83 ✅ aur 978 ÷ 100 = 9.78 ✅.\n\n12. (a) **False** ❌. 400 km par g lagbhag 8.7 m/s² hai (Part F). Astronaut tairte hain kyunki wo aur ISS free fall mein hain.\n    (b) **False** ❌. Simple model mein g centre ki taraf seedhi line mein **ghat'ta** hai aur centre par zero hai.\n    (c) **True** ✅. Mass matter ki matra hai, jagah se nahi badalta.\n    (d) **False** ❌. Mars par g = 3.7, Jupiter par 25 m/s²: Jupiter par weight zyada hai.\n    (e) **True** ✅. Poles centre ke paas hain aur wahan ghoomne ka asar nahi hota.\n\n13. Moon par g lagbhag 1.6 m/s² hai, Earth ka lagbhag chhathva hissa. Mass wahi hai, to weight (W = m g) chhathva hissa reh jaata hai. Jab astronaut kood ke liye zameen chhodta hai, usko neeche kheenchne wala force kam hai, to wo zyada der tak oopar jaata hai aur kai guna oonchi kood jaata hai (v² = 2 g h se, wahi chaal par h ∝ 1/g). Mass nahi badla, g badla.\n\n---\n\nYuvraj ne kaagaz par likha: \"ISS par g = 8.7, Jupiter par mera weight 2.6 guna, aur Earth ke centre par g = 0.\" Mr. Godbole bole, \"Dekha, ek hi Earth par g ek jaisa nahi hai, par kahin bhi poora zero bhi nahi (centre ko chhodkar). Ab agar hamein kisi cheez ko itna tez phenkna ho ki wo kabhi wapas hi na aaye?\"\n\n**Aage kya?** Topic 3 mein hum dekhenge **escape velocity**: cheez ko kitni tez phenkein ki wo Earth ki gravity se bach kar nikal jaaye, aur ye Chaand, Mars aur Jupiter par alag kyun hoti hai.",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. Find the acceleration due to gravity at a height of 100 km above the Earth's surface. (Use g = 9.8 m/s² at the surface and R = 6400 km.)\n\n2. A communication satellite is at a height of 36,000 km above the Earth's surface. Find g at that height, and say what fraction of the surface value it is. (g = 9.8 m/s², R = 6400 km.)\n\n3. A miner works 1 km below the surface of the Earth. Find g there and by how much it differs from the surface value. (g = 9.8 m/s², R = 6400 km. Use the g_d = g (1 − d/R) rule.)\n\n4. Find g at a depth equal to half the Earth's radius (d = R/2), taking g = 9.8 m/s² at the surface.\n\n5. On Mars, g is about 3.7 m/s². A student has a mass of 48 kg. Find her weight on the Earth and on Mars, and her mass on Mars.\n\n6. On Jupiter (at the cloud tops), g is about 25 m/s². Find the weight of a 44 kg student on the Earth and at Jupiter's cloud tops. How many times bigger is it on Jupiter?\n\n7. A mountaineer of mass 76 kg stands on a mountain peak 8 km above sea level. Find g there (take R = 6400 km), and the change in his weight compared with sea level. Comment on whether this change would be noticed.\n\n8. A person jumps 0.40 m high on the Earth. Using the same take-off speed, how high would she jump on Mars (g = 3.7 m/s²)? (Take g_E = 9.8 m/s² and ignore air.)\n\n9. At what height above the Earth's surface does g become half of its surface value? (R = 6400 km. Hint: use g_h / g = (R / (R + h))² = 1/2 and a square root.)\n\n10. At what depth below the surface does g fall to 7.84 m/s²? Take g = 9.8 m/s² and R = 6400 km.\n\n11. A 100 kg person is weighed using a spring balance at the North Pole and at the equator. Find the two readings (g_pole = 9.83 m/s², g_equator = 9.78 m/s²). Will a beam balance show a different mass at these two places?\n\n12. State True or False and correct the false statements:\n    (a) At the height of the ISS (about 400 km), g is zero.\n    (b) Inside the Earth, g increases steadily as we go towards the centre.\n    (c) A 1 kg object has the same mass on the Moon as on the Earth.\n    (d) The weight of an object on Mars is more than on Jupiter.\n    (e) The value of g at the poles is slightly greater than at the equator.\n\n13. Explain in your own words why astronauts hop to a great height on the Moon even though their mass is the same as on the Earth.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. **Given:** h = 100 km. **Formula:** g_h = g (R / (R + h))². **Substitution:** R + h = 6500 km. R/(R + h) = 6400 ÷ 6500 = 0.98462. Square = 0.96945. g_h = 9.8 × 0.96945 = **9.50 m/s²**. **Jaanch:** 9.50 < 9.8 ✅ aur 9.50 ÷ 9.8 = 0.969 ≈ (0.9846)² ✅. 100 km par g sirf lagbhag 3% kam hua.\n\n2. **Given:** h = 36,000 km. **Formula:** g_h = g (R / (R + h))². **Substitution:** R + h = 6400 + 36,000 = 42,400 km. R/(R + h) = 6400 ÷ 42,400 = 0.15094. Square = 0.022783. g_h = 9.8 × 0.022783 = **0.223 m/s²** (lagbhag 0.22). Hissa = 0.022783, yaani lagbhag **2.3%** surface ke g ka. **Jaanch:** Doori 6.625 guna hui (42,400 ÷ 6400), to g 1/6.625² = 0.0228 guna ✅. Ye zero nahi hai: aur Topic 4 mein dekhenge ki isi chhote g se satellite 24 ghante mein ek chakkar lagata hai.\n\n3. **Given:** d = 1 km. **Formula:** g_d = g (1 − d/R). **Substitution:** d/R = 1 ÷ 6400 = 0.00015625. g_d = 9.8 × (1 − 0.00015625) = 9.8 × 0.99984 = **9.7985 m/s²** (lagbhag 9.80). Farak = 9.8 − 9.7985 = **0.0015 m/s²**. **Jaanch:** Farak = g × d/R = 9.8 × 0.00015625 = 0.0015 ✅. Itna chhota farak koi nahi mehsoos kar sakta, isliye khaan mein bhi g = 9.8 maanna theek hai.\n\n4. **Given:** d = R/2. **Formula:** g_d = g (1 − d/R). **Substitution:** d/R = 1/2. g_d = 9.8 × (1 − 0.5) = 9.8 × 0.5 = **4.9 m/s²**. **Jaanch:** 4.9 ÷ 9.8 = 0.5 = 1 − 0.5 ✅. (d = 3200 km, aadhe raaste par aadha g.)\n\n5. **Given:** m = 48 kg, g_E = 9.8 m/s², g_Mars = 3.7 m/s². **Formula:** W = m g. **Substitution:** W_E = 48 × 9.8 = 470.4 N. W_Mars = 48 × 3.7 = 177.6 N. **Answer:** Earth par **470.4 N**, Mars par **177.6 N**, mass Mars par bhi **48 kg**. **Jaanch:** 177.6 ÷ 470.4 = 0.378 = 3.7 ÷ 9.8 ✅.\n\n6. **Given:** m = 44 kg, g_E = 9.8, g_J = 25 m/s². **Formula:** W = m g. **Substitution:** W_E = 44 × 9.8 = 431.2 N. W_J = 44 × 25 = 1100 N. Anupaat = 1100 ÷ 431.2 = 2.55. **Answer:** **431.2 N** aur **1100 N**; Jupiter par lagbhag **2.6 guna**. **Jaanch:** 25 ÷ 9.8 = 2.55 ✅. Mass dono jagah 44 kg hi hai.\n\n7. **Given:** m = 76 kg, h = 8 km. **Formula:** g_h = g (R / (R + h))². **Substitution:** R + h = 6408 km. R/(R + h) = 6400 ÷ 6408 = 0.998752. Square = 0.997505. g_h = 9.8 × 0.997505 = **9.7755 m/s²** (lagbhag 9.78). Sea level par W = 76 × 9.8 = 744.8 N. Peak par W = 76 × 9.7755 = 742.9 N. Farak = **lagbhag 1.9 N**. **Jaanch:** Farak ≈ 2h/R × W = 2 × 8 ÷ 6400 × 744.8 = 1.86 N ✅. Itna chhota farak (lagbhag 0.25%) bina tez instrument ke mehsoos nahi hoga. Yaad rakho: 8 km, 6400 km ke saamne bahut chhota hai.\n\n8. **Given:** h_E = 0.40 m, g_E = 9.8, g_M = 3.7 m/s². **Formula:** u² = 2 g h (wahi u dono jagah), to h ∝ 1/g. **Substitution:** h_Mars = h_E × g_E ÷ g_Mars = 0.40 × 9.8 ÷ 3.7 = 3.92 ÷ 3.7 = **1.06 m**. **Jaanch:** Takeoff speed u = √(2 × 9.8 × 0.40) = √7.84 = 2.8 m/s. Mars par h = u² ÷ (2 × 3.7) = 7.84 ÷ 7.4 = 1.06 m ✅.\n\n9. **Given:** g_h / g = 1/2. **Formula:** (R / (R + h))² = 1/2, to R / (R + h) = 1/√2. **Substitution:** R + h = √2 × R = 1.4142 × 6400 = 9051 km, to h = 9051 − 6400 = **lagbhag 2651 km** (yaani 0.414 R). **Jaanch:** g_h = 9.8 × (6400 ÷ 9051)² = 9.8 × (0.7071)² = 9.8 × 0.5 = 4.9 m/s² ✅.\n\n10. **Given:** g_d = 7.84 m/s², g = 9.8 m/s². **Formula:** g_d = g (1 − d/R), to 1 − d/R = g_d ÷ g. **Substitution:** 7.84 ÷ 9.8 = 0.8, to d/R = 1 − 0.8 = 0.2. d = 0.2 × 6400 = **1280 km**. **Jaanch:** 9.8 × (1 − 0.2) = 9.8 × 0.8 = 7.84 m/s² ✅.\n\n11. **Given:** m = 100 kg, g_pole = 9.83, g_eq = 9.78 m/s². **Formula:** W = m g. **Substitution:** W_pole = 100 × 9.83 = 983 N. W_eq = 100 × 9.78 = 978 N. **Answer:** spring balance **983 N** aur **978 N** (farak 5 N). Beam balance dono jagah **100 kg** hi dikhayegi, kyunki wo apne mass ko doosre known mass se compare karti hai (g dono taraf ek jaisa lagta hai). **Jaanch:** 983 ÷ 100 = 9.83 ✅ aur 978 ÷ 100 = 9.78 ✅.\n\n12. (a) **False** ❌. 400 km par g lagbhag 8.7 m/s² hai (Part F). Astronaut tairte hain kyunki wo aur ISS free fall mein hain.\n    (b) **False** ❌. Simple model mein g centre ki taraf seedhi line mein **ghat'ta** hai aur centre par zero hai.\n    (c) **True** ✅. Mass matter ki matra hai, jagah se nahi badalta.\n    (d) **False** ❌. Mars par g = 3.7, Jupiter par 25 m/s²: Jupiter par weight zyada hai.\n    (e) **True** ✅. Poles centre ke paas hain aur wahan ghoomne ka asar nahi hota.\n\n13. Moon par g lagbhag 1.6 m/s² hai, Earth ka lagbhag chhathva hissa. Mass wahi hai, to weight (W = m g) chhathva hissa reh jaata hai. Jab astronaut kood ke liye zameen chhodta hai, usko neeche kheenchne wala force kam hai, to wo zyada der tak oopar jaata hai aur kai guna oonchi kood jaata hai (v² = 2 g h se, wahi chaal par h ∝ 1/g). Mass nahi badla, g badla.\n\n---\n\nYuvraj ne kaagaz par likha: \"ISS par g = 8.7, Jupiter par mera weight 2.6 guna, aur Earth ke centre par g = 0.\" Mr. Godbole bole, \"Dekha, ek hi Earth par g ek jaisa nahi hai, par kahin bhi poora zero bhi nahi (centre ko chhodkar). Ab agar hamein kisi cheez ko itna tez phenkna ho ki wo kabhi wapas hi na aaye?\"\n\n**Aage kya?** Topic 3 mein hum dekhenge **escape velocity**: cheez ko kitni tez phenkein ki wo Earth ki gravity se bach kar nikal jaaye, aur ye Chaand, Mars aur Jupiter par alag kyun hoti hai."
          }
