          {
            "heading": "Part E: Meter: kitni bijli kharch hui?",
            "headingHinglish": "Part E: Meter: kitni bijli kharch hui?",
            "body": "Ghar ke entrance par **energy meter** hai (Class 9). Wo **kWh (units)** mein ginti karta hai, aur 1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ J. Purane **disc** wale meter mein ek chakri ghoomti hai jo jitni zyada power, utni tez ghoomti hai (iska raaz bhi Chapter 9 mein); **digital** meter number dikhata hai; **smart meter** wahi digital meter hai jo reading company ko door se bhej bhi sakta hai; **prepaid meter** mein pehle recharge karte hain (kai jagah balance khatam hone par bijli rok di jaati hai).\n\nReading padhne ka tareeka: **ab ki reading − pichhli reading = is dauran ke units**.\n\n```\n  Pichhla reading:   1 8 3 6 4   kWh\n  Is mahine ka:      1 8 6 0 1   kWh\n                     ---------\n  Farak:                 2 3 7   kWh = 237 units\n```\n\n> **Figure 5.4** – Meter ki do readings ka farak: is baar ki reading mein se pichhli reading ghatao.\n\n### Example 4: Reading aur slab wala bill\n\n**Given:** Pichhle mahine ki reading 18364 units aur is mahine ki 18601 units. Tariff (sirf is sawaal ke liye maan liya): pehle 100 units ₹3.40 per unit, usse upar ₹5.10 per unit. Fixed charge nahi jodte.\n**Find:** Units, aur bill.\n**Formula:** units = ab − pichhli; bill = (pehle 100) × ₹3.40 + (baaki) × ₹5.10.\n**Substitution:** units = 18601 − 18364 = 237 units. Pehle 100 units: 100 × ₹3.40 = ₹340. Baaki: 237 − 100 = 137 units, 137 × ₹5.10 = ₹698.70. Bill = ₹340 + ₹698.70 = ₹1038.70.\n**Answer:** 237 units aur ₹1038.70.\n**Jaanch:** 100 + 137 = 237 ✅; ₹340 + ₹698.70 = ₹1038.70 ✅\n\n### Ghar par adult ke saath: meter ki reading (sirf dekho)\n\nBade ke saath meter ke paas jao (meter ke dabbe ko **chhoona nahi**, kholna nahi). Aaj shaam ki reading likho aur do din baad usi samay ki dobara likho. Farak nikalo: do din ke units. Phir 2 se bhaag do: roz ke units. Bade se poochho ki is dauran ghar mein kya zyada chala.\n\n**Net metering** (recall): chhat ke panel ki bachi bijli grid mein jaati hai, to **net** units = jitna liya − jitna diya ([sources-of-energy-part4.md](../sources-of-energy/sources-of-energy-part4.md)).",
            "bodyHinglish": "Ghar ke entrance par **energy meter** hai (Class 9). Wo **kWh (units)** mein ginti karta hai, aur 1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ J. Purane **disc** wale meter mein ek chakri ghoomti hai jo jitni zyada power, utni tez ghoomti hai (iska raaz bhi Chapter 9 mein); **digital** meter number dikhata hai; **smart meter** wahi digital meter hai jo reading company ko door se bhej bhi sakta hai; **prepaid meter** mein pehle recharge karte hain (kai jagah balance khatam hone par bijli rok di jaati hai).\n\nReading padhne ka tareeka: **ab ki reading − pichhli reading = is dauran ke units**.\n\n```\n  Pichhla reading:   1 8 3 6 4   kWh\n  Is mahine ka:      1 8 6 0 1   kWh\n                     ---------\n  Farak:                 2 3 7   kWh = 237 units\n```\n\n> **Figure 5.4** – Meter ki do readings ka farak: is baar ki reading mein se pichhli reading ghatao.\n\n### Example 4: Reading aur slab wala bill\n\n**Given:** Pichhle mahine ki reading 18364 units aur is mahine ki 18601 units. Tariff (sirf is sawaal ke liye maan liya): pehle 100 units ₹3.40 per unit, usse upar ₹5.10 per unit. Fixed charge nahi jodte.\n**Find:** Units, aur bill.\n**Formula:** units = ab − pichhli; bill = (pehle 100) × ₹3.40 + (baaki) × ₹5.10.\n**Substitution:** units = 18601 − 18364 = 237 units. Pehle 100 units: 100 × ₹3.40 = ₹340. Baaki: 237 − 100 = 137 units, 137 × ₹5.10 = ₹698.70. Bill = ₹340 + ₹698.70 = ₹1038.70.\n**Answer:** 237 units aur ₹1038.70.\n**Jaanch:** 100 + 137 = 237 ✅; ₹340 + ₹698.70 = ₹1038.70 ✅\n\n### Ghar par adult ke saath: meter ki reading (sirf dekho)\n\nBade ke saath meter ke paas jao (meter ke dabbe ko **chhoona nahi**, kholna nahi). Aaj shaam ki reading likho aur do din baad usi samay ki dobara likho. Farak nikalo: do din ke units. Phir 2 se bhaag do: roz ke units. Bade se poochho ki is dauran ghar mein kya zyada chala.\n\n**Net metering** (recall): chhat ke panel ki bachi bijli grid mein jaati hai, to **net** units = jitna liya − jitna diya ([sources-of-energy-part4.md](../sources-of-energy/sources-of-energy-part4.md))."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part F: Bijli chali gayi: load shedding aur inverter",
            "headingHinglish": "Part F: Bijli chali gayi: load shedding aur inverter",
            "body": "Rutuja ne poochha: \"Mama, aaj bijli kyun gayi?\" Mama ne kaaran ginaye: line par kisi ped ka girna, kisi taar ya transformer mein kharabi, mausam, ya maintenance ka kaam. Kai baar network ki maang supply se zyada ho jaati hai to company kuch ilaakon ki bijli baari-baari se kaat deti hai: **load shedding**. (Ye hamesha nahi hota, aur jagah par nirbhar hai.)\n\nKai ghar **inverter** (battery wali) rakhte hain. Dhyan do: ghar ke board se inverter ka judna **electrician ka kaam** hai, aur uske beech **changeover switch** zaroori hai.\n\n```\n  Bijli company ki line --> [ Meter ] ----+\n                                          v\n                                 +------------------+\n                                 :  changeover      : -----> Ghar ka board\n                                 :  switch          :\n                                 +------------------+\n                                          :\n  Inverter + battery ---------------------+\n```\n\n> **Figure 5.5** – Changeover switch ghar ke board ko ek samay mein sirf ek hi source se jodta hai: company ki line (meter se hokar) ya inverter. Wo dono ko ek saath nahi jodta.\n\n**Kyun zaroori hai?** Agar inverter ki bijli bina changeover ke ghar ke taaron se hote hue gali ki line mein chali jaye, to jis line ko lineman ne band samajhkar chhua, wo ghar ki bijli se **zinda** ho sakti hai: lineman ke liye jaan ka khatra. Isliye ye kaam sirf electrician ka hai: student sirf itna jaane.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Rutuja ne poochha: \"Mama, aaj bijli kyun gayi?\" Mama ne kaaran ginaye: line par kisi ped ka girna, kisi taar ya transformer mein kharabi, mausam, ya maintenance ka kaam. Kai baar network ki maang supply se zyada ho jaati hai to company kuch ilaakon ki bijli baari-baari se kaat deti hai: **load shedding**. (Ye hamesha nahi hota, aur jagah par nirbhar hai.)\n\nKai ghar **inverter** (battery wali) rakhte hain. Dhyan do: ghar ke board se inverter ka judna **electrician ka kaam** hai, aur uske beech **changeover switch** zaroori hai.\n\n```\n  Bijli company ki line --> [ Meter ] ----+\n                                          v\n                                 +------------------+\n                                 :  changeover      : -----> Ghar ka board\n                                 :  switch          :\n                                 +------------------+\n                                          :\n  Inverter + battery ---------------------+\n```\n\n> **Figure 5.5** – Changeover switch ghar ke board ko ek samay mein sirf ek hi source se jodta hai: company ki line (meter se hokar) ya inverter. Wo dono ko ek saath nahi jodta.\n\n**Kyun zaroori hai?** Agar inverter ki bijli bina changeover ke ghar ke taaron se hote hue gali ki line mein chali jaye, to jis line ko lineman ne band samajhkar chhua, wo ghar ki bijli se **zinda** ho sakti hai: lineman ke liye jaan ka khatra. Isliye ye kaam sirf electrician ka hai: student sirf itna jaane."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Wapas khambe ke paas",
            "headingHinglish": "Wapas khambe ke paas",
            "body": "Ab Rutuja ke saare sawaalon ka jawab hai. Roshni **bijli-ghar** se aati hai, jo generator ke zariye lagbhag 11 kV par banti hai. Voltage **badhaakar** (lagbhag 132 se 765 kV) lambi lines mein bheji jaati hai, taaki current kam ho aur I² R loss bahut kam. Substation se gali tak lagbhag 11 kV, aur **pole transformer** use **415 V teen-phase** (lagbhag) aur ghar ke liye **230 V** (lagbhag) bana deta hai. Isliye khambon ke taar motey aur ooncha voltage waale, par ghar ke taar patle.\n\nRutuja ne ek line likhi: \"Jitna oonchha voltage, utna chhota current, utna kam I² R.\" Mama ne kaha, \"Yahi poore grid ka ek-line saar hai.\"",
            "bodyHinglish": "Ab Rutuja ke saare sawaalon ka jawab hai. Roshni **bijli-ghar** se aati hai, jo generator ke zariye lagbhag 11 kV par banti hai. Voltage **badhaakar** (lagbhag 132 se 765 kV) lambi lines mein bheji jaati hai, taaki current kam ho aur I² R loss bahut kam. Substation se gali tak lagbhag 11 kV, aur **pole transformer** use **415 V teen-phase** (lagbhag) aur ghar ke liye **230 V** (lagbhag) bana deta hai. Isliye khambon ke taar motey aur ooncha voltage waale, par ghar ke taar patle.\n\nRutuja ne ek line likhi: \"Jitna oonchha voltage, utna chhota current, utna kam I² R.\" Mama ne kaha, \"Yahi poore grid ka ek-line saar hai.\""
          },
          {
            "heading": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "body": "**Galti 1: \"Line par bahut ooncha voltage hai, to ghar mein bhi wahi voltage aata hoga.\"** Demonstration: Rutuja ne socha ki 765 kV ki lagbhag line se ghar ki bijli seedhi aati hai, to socket mein bhi 765 kV aata hoga. Par 765 000 V ÷ 230 V = 3326 guna: itne voltage par ghar ka bulb ek pal mein jal jaata aur insaan ke liye jaan ka khatra hota. Raaste mein **substation aur pole transformer** voltage ghatate hain (765 kV, ya 400 kV, se 11 kV, phir 415 V aur 230 V). Ghar ka voltage **ghar ke liye tay** hota hai: 230 V.\n\n**Galti 2: \"Bijli ki line mein bijli koi loss nahi hoti.\"** Demonstration: Mama ne ek line ka udaharan diya: V = 132 kV, I = 50 A, R = 3 Ω. Bheji gayi power P = V I = 132 000 V × 50 A = 6 600 000 W = 6.6 MW. Line loss = I² R = (50 A)² × 3 Ω = 2500 A² × 3 Ω = 7500 W = 7.5 kW. Ye P ka 7500 ÷ 6 600 000 × 100 = 0.114 percent hai: bahut kam, par **zero nahi**, aur ye din-raat chalti hai: 24 ghante mein 7.5 kW × 24 h = 180 kWh. Isliye loss ko **kam** kiya jaata hai (oonchha voltage, mota taar), khatam nahi.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Galti 1: \"Line par bahut ooncha voltage hai, to ghar mein bhi wahi voltage aata hoga.\"** Demonstration: Rutuja ne socha ki 765 kV ki lagbhag line se ghar ki bijli seedhi aati hai, to socket mein bhi 765 kV aata hoga. Par 765 000 V ÷ 230 V = 3326 guna: itne voltage par ghar ka bulb ek pal mein jal jaata aur insaan ke liye jaan ka khatra hota. Raaste mein **substation aur pole transformer** voltage ghatate hain (765 kV, ya 400 kV, se 11 kV, phir 415 V aur 230 V). Ghar ka voltage **ghar ke liye tay** hota hai: 230 V.\n\n**Galti 2: \"Bijli ki line mein bijli koi loss nahi hoti.\"** Demonstration: Mama ne ek line ka udaharan diya: V = 132 kV, I = 50 A, R = 3 Ω. Bheji gayi power P = V I = 132 000 V × 50 A = 6 600 000 W = 6.6 MW. Line loss = I² R = (50 A)² × 3 Ω = 2500 A² × 3 Ω = 7500 W = 7.5 kW. Ye P ka 7500 ÷ 6 600 000 × 100 = 0.114 percent hai: bahut kam, par **zero nahi**, aur ye din-raat chalti hai: 24 ghante mein 7.5 kW × 24 h = 180 kWh. Isliye loss ko **kam** kiya jaata hai (oonchha voltage, mota taar), khatam nahi."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- Bijli ka safar: **bijli-ghar** (lagbhag 11 kV) → **step-up transformer** → **transmission line** (132, 220, 400, 765 kV lagbhag) → **grid substation** (step-down) → **distribution line** (lagbhag 11 kV) → **pole transformer** → ghar (230 V).\n- Ek hi power P = V I ke liye V badhao to I = P ÷ V ghatta hai, aur **line loss I² R = P² R ÷ V²**: voltage 10 guna to loss 1 ÷ 100.\n- Ooncha voltage hamesha aur oonchha nahi: tower, insulator aur kharcha seema tay karte hain.\n- Transmission AC mein isliye ki **transformer** AC voltage badhata-ghatata hai (raaz Chapter 9).\n- Grid lagbhag **50 Hz**: current 1 second mein lagbhag 100 baar disha badalta hai.\n- Pole transformer ke output par **teen live (R, Y, B) aur ek neutral**; do live ke beech lagbhag 400 V, live aur neutral ke beech lagbhag 230 V (400 ÷ √3).\n- Meter: **ab − pichhli reading = units**; smart aur prepaid meter naam bas; net metering ka hisaab net units.\n- **Load shedding** supply-maang ka antar; **changeover switch** ke bina inverter ghar mein mat jodna (electrician ka kaam).\n- Khambon, transformer aur taaron ke paas kabhi nahi jaana: sirf door se dekhna.",
            "bodyHinglish": "- Bijli ka safar: **bijli-ghar** (lagbhag 11 kV) → **step-up transformer** → **transmission line** (132, 220, 400, 765 kV lagbhag) → **grid substation** (step-down) → **distribution line** (lagbhag 11 kV) → **pole transformer** → ghar (230 V).\n- Ek hi power P = V I ke liye V badhao to I = P ÷ V ghatta hai, aur **line loss I² R = P² R ÷ V²**: voltage 10 guna to loss 1 ÷ 100.\n- Ooncha voltage hamesha aur oonchha nahi: tower, insulator aur kharcha seema tay karte hain.\n- Transmission AC mein isliye ki **transformer** AC voltage badhata-ghatata hai (raaz Chapter 9).\n- Grid lagbhag **50 Hz**: current 1 second mein lagbhag 100 baar disha badalta hai.\n- Pole transformer ke output par **teen live (R, Y, B) aur ek neutral**; do live ke beech lagbhag 400 V, live aur neutral ke beech lagbhag 230 V (400 ÷ √3).\n- Meter: **ab − pichhli reading = units**; smart aur prepaid meter naam bas; net metering ka hisaab net units.\n- **Load shedding** supply-maang ka antar; **changeover switch** ke bina inverter ghar mein mat jodna (electrician ka kaam).\n- Khambon, transformer aur taaron ke paas kabhi nahi jaana: sirf door se dekhna."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "(Use 230 V as the household supply. Treat the line resistance as constant and ignore the voltage drop along the line.)\n\n1. A power of 6 MW is sent over a line of total resistance 12 Ω at 11 kV. Find the current, the line loss and the percentage of the power lost.\n2. The same 6 MW goes through the same 12 Ω line at 110 kV. Find the current and the line loss. By what factor is the loss smaller than in Question 1?\n3. A factory needs 25 MW through a line of resistance 40 Ω at 220 kV. Find the current, the loss in kW and the percentage loss.\n4. Use the same 25 MW and 40 Ω as in Question 3, but suppose the line is run at 11 kV. Find the loss the formula gives and compare it with 25 MW. What does this tell you about sending this power at 11 kV?\n5. An energy meter reads 07426 units at the start of a month and 07688 units at the end. The tariff is ₹4.75 per unit plus a fixed charge of ₹85. Find the units used and the total bill.\n6. A prepaid meter has a balance of ₹372 and the tariff is ₹6.20 per unit. A water pump of 0.8 kW runs for 2.5 hours every day. How many units can the balance buy, and for how many days will it last if the pump is the only load?\n7. Between two live wires of a three-phase supply the voltage is about 400 V. Starting from 230 V between a live wire and the neutral, check by calculation that 230 V is consistent with 400 V. Then say which voltage a single-phase house gets and which a factory motor between two live wires gets.\n8. The mains frequency is 50 Hz. How many times does the current reverse direction in 3 minutes? In a country with 60 Hz, how many times does it reverse in one second, and what is the time of one cycle?\n9. State True or False with a reason. (a) The 400 kV transmission line supplies a house socket directly. (b) For the same power, sending at a higher voltage means a smaller current. (c) If the voltage on a line is doubled, the line loss becomes half. (d) A smart meter can send the reading to the company.\n10. Arrange in the correct order: pole transformer, generator, grid substation, step-up transformer, house, transmission line, distribution line.\n11. For the same power and the same line, find the factor by which the line loss changes when the transmission voltage is raised from 11 kV to 220 kV.\n12. A line carries 6 MW over a resistance of 12 Ω. The company wants the loss to be at most 3 percent of the power sent. Find the largest allowed current and the smallest voltage needed.\n13. In two lines each, explain (a) why a house inverter must go through a changeover switch fitted by an electrician, and (b) why a lineman, and not a student, works on a pole transformer.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. **Given:** P = 6 MW = 6 000 000 W, R = 12 Ω, V = 11 kV = 11 000 V. **Find:** I, loss, percent. **Formula:** I = P ÷ V; loss = I² R. **Substitution:** I = 6 000 000 W ÷ 11 000 V = **545.5 A**; loss = (545.5 A)² × 12 Ω = 297 520 A² × 12 Ω = 3 570 000 W ≈ **3.57 MW**; percent = 3.57 MW ÷ 6 MW × 100 = **59.5 percent**. **Jaanch:** 545.5 A × 11 000 V = 6.0 MW ✅. (Ye bahut bada loss hai: asli mein itne kam voltage par itni power is line se bheji hi nahi jaati.)\n2. **Substitution:** I = 6 000 000 W ÷ 110 000 V = **54.55 A**; loss = (54.55 A)² × 12 Ω = 2975 A² × 12 Ω = 35 700 W ≈ **35.7 kW**. Factor = 3 570 000 ÷ 35 700 = **100**. **Jaanch:** voltage 110 ÷ 11 = 10 guna, to loss 10² = 100 guna kam ✅.\n3. I = 25 000 000 W ÷ 220 000 V = **113.6 A**; loss = (113.6 A)² × 40 Ω = 12 913 A² × 40 Ω = 516 500 W ≈ **516.5 kW**; percent = 516.5 kW ÷ 25 000 kW × 100 = **2.07 percent**. **Jaanch:** 113.6 A × 220 000 V = 25.0 MW ✅.\n4. I = 25 000 000 W ÷ 11 000 V = 2272.7 A; loss = (2272.7 A)² × 40 Ω = 5 165 289 A² × 40 Ω = 206 600 000 W ≈ 206.6 MW. Ye 25 MW se bahut zyada hai: **826 percent**. Iska matlab: 11 kV par itni power us line se bheji hi nahi ja sakti (line ke resistance par hi bahut zyada voltage gir jaata; 2272.7 A × 40 Ω = 90 909 V, jo 11 000 V se bahut zyada hai). Isliye lambi, badi power ke liye ooncha voltage zaroori hai. **Jaanch:** 90 909 V ÷ 11 000 V = 8.26 ✅ (wahi 826 percent ka sanket).\n5. Units = 7688 − 7426 = **262 units**. Energy bill = 262 × ₹4.75 = ₹1244.50; fixed charge ₹85; total = ₹1244.50 + ₹85 = **₹1329.50**. **Jaanch:** 262 × 4.75 = 1244.5 ✅; 7426 + 262 = 7688 ✅.\n6. Units = ₹372 ÷ ₹6.20 per unit = **60 units**. Pump roz = 0.8 kW × 2.5 h = 2 kWh. Din = 60 kWh ÷ 2 kWh per din = **30 din**. **Jaanch:** 30 × 2 kWh × ₹6.20 = ₹372 ✅.\n7. 400 ÷ √3 = 400 ÷ 1.732 = 230.9 V ≈ 230 V ✅ (ulta: 230 × 1.732 = 398.4 V ≈ 400 V; fark 400 − 398.4 = 1.6 V, lagbhag 0.4 percent). Single-phase ghar ko ek live aur neutral se **230 V** (lagbhag); factory motor ko do live taaron ke beech **400 V** (lagbhag).\n8. 3 min = 180 s. Reversals = 100 per s × 180 s = **18 000**. 60 Hz par: 2 × 60 = **120 baar per second**, ek cycle ka samay T = 1 ÷ 60 Hz = **0.0167 s**. **Jaanch:** 100 × 180 = 18 000 ✅; 60 cycle × 0.0167 s = 1.0 s ✅.\n9. (a) **False** ❌: 400 kV line pehle substation aur pole transformer se guzarti hai, tab 230 V ghar tak aata hai. (b) **True** ✅: P = V I, V badhe to I = P ÷ V ghatta hai. (c) **False** ❌: loss V² ke ulte anupaat mein hota hai, to voltage double hone par loss **ek-chauthai (1 ÷ 4)**. (d) **True** ✅: smart meter reading door se bhej sakta hai.\n10. **generator → step-up transformer → transmission line → grid substation → distribution line → pole transformer → house.**\n11. Factor = (220 ÷ 11)² = 20² = **400 guna kam** (loss 1 ÷ 400 ho jaata hai). **Jaanch:** V ka anupaat 20 hai, to I ek-bees-va (1 ÷ 20) aur loss 1 ÷ 20² = 1 ÷ 400 ✅. (Q1 aur Q2 mein anupaat 10 tha aur factor 100 mila tha: wahi niyam.)\n12. **Given:** P = 6 MW = 6 000 000 W, R = 12 Ω, loss ≤ 3 percent. **Formula:** loss = I² R; V = P ÷ I. **Substitution:** Loss_max = 0.03 × 6 000 000 W = 180 000 W; I² = 180 000 W ÷ 12 Ω = 15 000 A²; I = **122.5 A**; V = 6 000 000 W ÷ 122.5 A = 48 980 V ≈ **49 kV**. **Jaanch:** (122.5 A)² × 12 Ω = 15 000 × 12 = 180 000 W ✅. Isliye 11 kV (Q1) bahut kam hai, 110 kV (Q2) kaafi hai.\n13. (a) Changeover switch ghar ko ek samay mein ek hi source se jodta hai, to inverter ki bijli gali ki line mein wapas nahi ja sakti, jise lineman band samajhkar kaam kar raha ho. Wiring electrician ka kaam hai. (b) Transformer aur line par lagbhag 11 kV (ya zyada) hota hai, jo jaan le sakta hai; lineman ko training, helmet, dastaane aur line band karne ke niyam aate hain, student ko door se dekhna hai.\n\n---\n\nAb tum jaante ho ki socket ke peeche kya hai: bijli-ghar se lagbhag 11 kV, step-up se ooncha voltage, lambi line mein kam current aur kam loss, substation aur pole transformer se 230 V, aur ghar ka meter jo hisaab rakhta hai. Rutuja ne ghar lautkar apne ghar ke khambe ko door se dekha aur kagaz par poora safar bana diya: \"Ab bijli mere liye jaadu nahi, hisaab hai.\"\n\n**Aage kya?** Topic 6 mein hum **sab kuch mila kar** dekhenge: fuse, MCB, RCCB aur earthing mein kaun kya bachata hai, kaun sa formula kab, mile-jule numerical, bachi hui galtiyan aur poore chapter ka saar.",
            "bodyHinglish": "(Use 230 V as the household supply. Treat the line resistance as constant and ignore the voltage drop along the line.)\n\n1. A power of 6 MW is sent over a line of total resistance 12 Ω at 11 kV. Find the current, the line loss and the percentage of the power lost.\n2. The same 6 MW goes through the same 12 Ω line at 110 kV. Find the current and the line loss. By what factor is the loss smaller than in Question 1?\n3. A factory needs 25 MW through a line of resistance 40 Ω at 220 kV. Find the current, the loss in kW and the percentage loss.\n4. Use the same 25 MW and 40 Ω as in Question 3, but suppose the line is run at 11 kV. Find the loss the formula gives and compare it with 25 MW. What does this tell you about sending this power at 11 kV?\n5. An energy meter reads 07426 units at the start of a month and 07688 units at the end. The tariff is ₹4.75 per unit plus a fixed charge of ₹85. Find the units used and the total bill.\n6. A prepaid meter has a balance of ₹372 and the tariff is ₹6.20 per unit. A water pump of 0.8 kW runs for 2.5 hours every day. How many units can the balance buy, and for how many days will it last if the pump is the only load?\n7. Between two live wires of a three-phase supply the voltage is about 400 V. Starting from 230 V between a live wire and the neutral, check by calculation that 230 V is consistent with 400 V. Then say which voltage a single-phase house gets and which a factory motor between two live wires gets.\n8. The mains frequency is 50 Hz. How many times does the current reverse direction in 3 minutes? In a country with 60 Hz, how many times does it reverse in one second, and what is the time of one cycle?\n9. State True or False with a reason. (a) The 400 kV transmission line supplies a house socket directly. (b) For the same power, sending at a higher voltage means a smaller current. (c) If the voltage on a line is doubled, the line loss becomes half. (d) A smart meter can send the reading to the company.\n10. Arrange in the correct order: pole transformer, generator, grid substation, step-up transformer, house, transmission line, distribution line.\n11. For the same power and the same line, find the factor by which the line loss changes when the transmission voltage is raised from 11 kV to 220 kV.\n12. A line carries 6 MW over a resistance of 12 Ω. The company wants the loss to be at most 3 percent of the power sent. Find the largest allowed current and the smallest voltage needed.\n13. In two lines each, explain (a) why a house inverter must go through a changeover switch fitted by an electrician, and (b) why a lineman, and not a student, works on a pole transformer.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. **Given:** P = 6 MW = 6 000 000 W, R = 12 Ω, V = 11 kV = 11 000 V. **Find:** I, loss, percent. **Formula:** I = P ÷ V; loss = I² R. **Substitution:** I = 6 000 000 W ÷ 11 000 V = **545.5 A**; loss = (545.5 A)² × 12 Ω = 297 520 A² × 12 Ω = 3 570 000 W ≈ **3.57 MW**; percent = 3.57 MW ÷ 6 MW × 100 = **59.5 percent**. **Jaanch:** 545.5 A × 11 000 V = 6.0 MW ✅. (Ye bahut bada loss hai: asli mein itne kam voltage par itni power is line se bheji hi nahi jaati.)\n2. **Substitution:** I = 6 000 000 W ÷ 110 000 V = **54.55 A**; loss = (54.55 A)² × 12 Ω = 2975 A² × 12 Ω = 35 700 W ≈ **35.7 kW**. Factor = 3 570 000 ÷ 35 700 = **100**. **Jaanch:** voltage 110 ÷ 11 = 10 guna, to loss 10² = 100 guna kam ✅.\n3. I = 25 000 000 W ÷ 220 000 V = **113.6 A**; loss = (113.6 A)² × 40 Ω = 12 913 A² × 40 Ω = 516 500 W ≈ **516.5 kW**; percent = 516.5 kW ÷ 25 000 kW × 100 = **2.07 percent**. **Jaanch:** 113.6 A × 220 000 V = 25.0 MW ✅.\n4. I = 25 000 000 W ÷ 11 000 V = 2272.7 A; loss = (2272.7 A)² × 40 Ω = 5 165 289 A² × 40 Ω = 206 600 000 W ≈ 206.6 MW. Ye 25 MW se bahut zyada hai: **826 percent**. Iska matlab: 11 kV par itni power us line se bheji hi nahi ja sakti (line ke resistance par hi bahut zyada voltage gir jaata; 2272.7 A × 40 Ω = 90 909 V, jo 11 000 V se bahut zyada hai). Isliye lambi, badi power ke liye ooncha voltage zaroori hai. **Jaanch:** 90 909 V ÷ 11 000 V = 8.26 ✅ (wahi 826 percent ka sanket).\n5. Units = 7688 − 7426 = **262 units**. Energy bill = 262 × ₹4.75 = ₹1244.50; fixed charge ₹85; total = ₹1244.50 + ₹85 = **₹1329.50**. **Jaanch:** 262 × 4.75 = 1244.5 ✅; 7426 + 262 = 7688 ✅.\n6. Units = ₹372 ÷ ₹6.20 per unit = **60 units**. Pump roz = 0.8 kW × 2.5 h = 2 kWh. Din = 60 kWh ÷ 2 kWh per din = **30 din**. **Jaanch:** 30 × 2 kWh × ₹6.20 = ₹372 ✅.\n7. 400 ÷ √3 = 400 ÷ 1.732 = 230.9 V ≈ 230 V ✅ (ulta: 230 × 1.732 = 398.4 V ≈ 400 V; fark 400 − 398.4 = 1.6 V, lagbhag 0.4 percent). Single-phase ghar ko ek live aur neutral se **230 V** (lagbhag); factory motor ko do live taaron ke beech **400 V** (lagbhag).\n8. 3 min = 180 s. Reversals = 100 per s × 180 s = **18 000**. 60 Hz par: 2 × 60 = **120 baar per second**, ek cycle ka samay T = 1 ÷ 60 Hz = **0.0167 s**. **Jaanch:** 100 × 180 = 18 000 ✅; 60 cycle × 0.0167 s = 1.0 s ✅.\n9. (a) **False** ❌: 400 kV line pehle substation aur pole transformer se guzarti hai, tab 230 V ghar tak aata hai. (b) **True** ✅: P = V I, V badhe to I = P ÷ V ghatta hai. (c) **False** ❌: loss V² ke ulte anupaat mein hota hai, to voltage double hone par loss **ek-chauthai (1 ÷ 4)**. (d) **True** ✅: smart meter reading door se bhej sakta hai.\n10. **generator → step-up transformer → transmission line → grid substation → distribution line → pole transformer → house.**\n11. Factor = (220 ÷ 11)² = 20² = **400 guna kam** (loss 1 ÷ 400 ho jaata hai). **Jaanch:** V ka anupaat 20 hai, to I ek-bees-va (1 ÷ 20) aur loss 1 ÷ 20² = 1 ÷ 400 ✅. (Q1 aur Q2 mein anupaat 10 tha aur factor 100 mila tha: wahi niyam.)\n12. **Given:** P = 6 MW = 6 000 000 W, R = 12 Ω, loss ≤ 3 percent. **Formula:** loss = I² R; V = P ÷ I. **Substitution:** Loss_max = 0.03 × 6 000 000 W = 180 000 W; I² = 180 000 W ÷ 12 Ω = 15 000 A²; I = **122.5 A**; V = 6 000 000 W ÷ 122.5 A = 48 980 V ≈ **49 kV**. **Jaanch:** (122.5 A)² × 12 Ω = 15 000 × 12 = 180 000 W ✅. Isliye 11 kV (Q1) bahut kam hai, 110 kV (Q2) kaafi hai.\n13. (a) Changeover switch ghar ko ek samay mein ek hi source se jodta hai, to inverter ki bijli gali ki line mein wapas nahi ja sakti, jise lineman band samajhkar kaam kar raha ho. Wiring electrician ka kaam hai. (b) Transformer aur line par lagbhag 11 kV (ya zyada) hota hai, jo jaan le sakta hai; lineman ko training, helmet, dastaane aur line band karne ke niyam aate hain, student ko door se dekhna hai.\n\n---\n\nAb tum jaante ho ki socket ke peeche kya hai: bijli-ghar se lagbhag 11 kV, step-up se ooncha voltage, lambi line mein kam current aur kam loss, substation aur pole transformer se 230 V, aur ghar ka meter jo hisaab rakhta hai. Rutuja ne ghar lautkar apne ghar ke khambe ko door se dekha aur kagaz par poora safar bana diya: \"Ab bijli mere liye jaadu nahi, hisaab hai.\"\n\n**Aage kya?** Topic 6 mein hum **sab kuch mila kar** dekhenge: fuse, MCB, RCCB aur earthing mein kaun kya bachata hai, kaun sa formula kab, mile-jule numerical, bachi hui galtiyan aur poore chapter ka saar."
          }
